[UTCDate "2026.02.16"]
[UTCTime "18:12:55"]
[Orientation "black"]

{ After few years of testing, thinking and analyzing, I think I've finally found the best opening for black, given goal of improvement, fun theory and playability. In this chapter, I would like to show you why I decided to recommend to you this exact opening as I think there is one strong contender for the spot of "Best Sicilian for ambitious Club Players".
Now, I had few goals I wanted to achive with this repertoire
1) Its sound - there are no lines where I am not sure if engine in 5 years will find some idea that will refute the line
2) Its intuitive - we can play well even if we don't remember the exact moves. This means that we don't have to go for the only moves for 20 moves straight
3) its simple - white has limited number of critical tries or at least we just need to know what set-up to pick out of some "box of set-ups" we have in a given structure
4) its rich - we get many different, fluid pawn structures so that we can train everything chess has to offer
5) its dynamic - we get dynamic games. That doesn't mean every-single-game looks like a game played by Tal, just that we always have an option for having a fun game
Now, I think I've found an opening which checks all these boxes. Also, this sicilian has one feature I really struggle at - winning better complex positional late middlegames (I know this sounds like a buzzword, but here me out). Here, we often get (especially in Richter Rauzer) better middlegame positions, but its quite hard to win them. In order to do so, we must play active, but toned positional chess. I struggle with that and I think this opening may help me with it (and so it may help you) } 1. e4 c5! { Well, why Sicilian defence? The answer is that it is perhaps the most flexibly, rich and sound opening out there. Everything else is just a subpar in at least of the these regards. If we take a look at
- 1... e5, its just way less dynamic and some lines (especially in Ruy Lopez) go 20 moves deep. And one thing I learnt lately is that on my level (2150+ chess.com rapid) people are buying and studying these 800+ variations Chessable courses.
- 1... e6 is fund opening, but it seems to me that some of the new found gambits in advanced French, some 3. Nc3 lines and even Tarrash just give white a fine game provided they know what they are doing. It's still a great opening and as a backup think, I think its my favourite to the Sicilian, however its just not as sound as the sicilian.
- 1... c6 enters one of the most played nowadays defences. For me, its a low hanging fruit which may give you great results, but won't teach you how to play chess, just how to react. It seems to me that if you play only this opening, then you plateau at some point because you were never taught how to play dynamic, imbalanced positions (as in Caro Kann, you can pretty much always go for either c5 or f6 and call it a day.
Everything else its its not even remotely as sound as sicilian. } 2. Nf3 d6 { Of all Sicilians, why 2... d6? Well
- 2... Nc6 runs into Rossolimo with 3. Bb5. This is of course playable as black, but we need to dedicate a large chunk of our studying time for this line, which is arguably even better than the open sicilian. In comparison, 3. Bb5+ here is a lot less potent as we have 3... Bd7 with easy game to follow.
- 2... e6 is great. This is the Sicilian I played for like 2 or more years. My only problem with it is that some lines are either really engine like (in Four Knight Sicilian) or there are just lines which engine shows are quite good for white and white's score in them is insane (and as I said, nowadays people at my level and above study these massive SuperGM courses). Also, this sicilian is perhaps the one where you often can only go for one structure. Sure there is a lot of flexibility in terms of piece play, but white has just as much freedom in their set-up and you pretty much always go for scheveningen or hedgehog (its quite rare that we see boleslavsky structure in e6 sicilians e.g. in Nb5 & Bf4 in Taimanov or in some lines of Bd3 Kan, but in the latter you can omit that if you want to). In comparison, in these sicilians we can get scheveningen, boleslavsky and dragon sicilians (and sometimes even maroczy bind, if white goes for moscow variation). Also, we can often choose what structure we want to play. Sure, we need to learn more but I think its a feature, not a drawback as we want to improve. Now, you could argue that scheveningen often turns into the boleslavsky structure and you would have a point, but we get that almost exclusively if white already has played f4. Still, I think there is greater flexibility and practical richer spectrum of positions in Classical Sicilian. } 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { Well, the last two moves are pretty much the only playable ones. We need to take on d4, its the main strategic point behind the sicilian and we play 4... Nf6 so that white kinda has to play 5. Nc3 which does not allow them to go for the maroczy bind structure. } 5. Nc3 { Here, we have 3 main options (4, but the Scheveningen is never played nowadays due to Keres Attack). Let's analyze the two I haven't picked first
- Najdorf Sicilian with 5... a6 is perhaps the most well know, best studied opening in chess. Fischer, Kasparov, Magnus everyone plays it at the top level. But the move 5...a6 as much as it prepares 6... e5 (so that there is no Bb5+) give a lot of flexibility to white and in the subsequent position, white has as many as 5 main moves and at least twice as many playable alternatives. All of these moves have their own ideas, own forcing variations and if we want to play Najdorf in modern day and age, we should know a bit about everyone of them. Sure, Najdorf is rich, flexible and sound, but its not practical.
- Dragon Sicilian with 5... g6 on the other hand is the most fun sicilian out of all. Its a great opening, we put our bishop on the long diagonal, we have healthy structure, easy to execute plans. There is just one problem - The Yugoslav Attack. It is the most theoretically challenging response to the Dragon Sicilian. Sure, in blitz and rapid black has great chances, but to play this well in classical chess or at higher level, we need to know quite a lot of theory to make it work and if someone knows we will play into this, then its quite easy to prepare against
So we have established that Najdorf is great for its flexibility and soundess and Dragon Sicilian is great, but the Yugoslav exists. The question is, can we eat the cookie and have the cookie? The answer is, yes. } { [%csl Gg6,Ga6,Bc6][%cal Gg7g6,Ga7a6,Bb8c6] } 5... Nc6!! { The Classical Sicilian. Idea is that we want white to commit to a set-up which wouldn't be useful if white were to go for the Yugoslav Attack. If they play e.g. 6. Be2 then 6... g6! lands us in the Dragon Sicilian. If we were to play the same idea but in Najdorf, we would have the pawn on a6 and the knight back on b8, which means we would just waste a tempo as a6 isn't always useful in the Dragon and Nc6 is pretty much always useful in the Dragon. And if they go for the Yugoslav attack with 6. Be3 we h ave 6... Ng4! which just works great. If they try to prepare this with 6. f3 e5! is a great move. Now we go for quick d5 and white doesn't have anything in these lines. Also, it is worth noting that we do not have to go for the Dragon set-up. We often can go for the either Boleslavsky Structure or the Scheveningen and white doesn't have as many sidelines as in Najdorf as Nc6 is just far more dynamic move that a6.
In terms of soundness, it has been played at the top level by Anand, Prag, Spassky against Fischer, Shankland and others. Modern engines show that this opening is sound too.
Okay, so we got an easy, not that theoretical, sound, rich and flexible opening - were is the catch? The answer is the 6. Bg5! which is Richter Rauzer. It is the most challenging variation against the Classical. If white plays this move against the Najdorf, we almost always play Nbd7, the knight doesn't sit well on c6. On the other hand, we cannot really go for the Dragon as after 6... g6 7. Bxf6 is a problem. It is playable, but white has damaged our pawn structure so its a bit problematic. Now, we have many extremely potent sidelines in the Rauzer, which are great for bullet, blitz and rapid. If we need a sound line, which gives us equality for classical chess, there are lines which provide this too. White objectively maybe has a bit more pleasant position according to the engine (Najdorf is a bit more favoured than Classical), but in return we get playability, flexibility, tricks and traps combined with extremely uncommon for white position. All in all, I think its a good trade. } *


[Event "Classical Sicilian: Open Sicilian: Richter Rauzer"]
[Date "2026.09.16"]
