[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B67"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Richter-Rauzer Variation, Neo-Modern Variation"]
[StudyName "Classical Sicilian"]
[ChapterName "Open Sicilian: Richter Rauzer"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/TJY2SPbI/HKXiEnu0"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/zumek"]
[UTCDate "2026.09.16"]
[UTCTime "17:00:44"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bg5! { This is the beginning of the Rauzer Variation. White idea is that they want to double our pawns and go for quick queen's side castle. What while will often do is they will go for wierd English Attack, but where their bishop in on g5. Now, as I said in the introduction, this is the best line against our set-up. However, we have many great and tricky sidelines, which work like a charm especially if our opponent isn't well versed in open Sicilian.
Here, I will recommend you a line which I believe gives us both practical chances and its sound. However, if there is an alternative, I will tell you about it so if you ever need an alternative, you know where to look. } { [%csl Bf6,Gd2,Gc1][%cal Bg5f6,Gd1d2,Ge1c1] } 6... e6 { We make sure they cannot take our knight and double our pawns (for now). There is one great alternative, 6... Bd7 with subsequent 7... Rc8, 8... Nxd4 and 9... Qa5. It seems to be quite sound and really tricky, so if you need an alternative, feel free to investigate this line. Also, black wins more than white there. There is even 6... g6?! which became quite popular lately and 6... Qb6 which is maybe a bit dubious but gives us an easy game and if we play against someone who is a lot weaker, then this could be a nice suprise. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gd8f6] } 7. Qd2 { One of three main moves. This one just defends the bishop and prepares O-O-O.
Now, what I want you to know is that if they play any weird more, then there is a high chance that h6 is a great move. The idea is that after h6, white must move their bishop. If they go to e3, then fine, there is no pressure on our knight. If they go to h4, then we have tactics with Nxe4! as the bishop is undefended. If they take, we get the bishop pair, so it cannot be this bad. Also, after Bh5 g5?! is often quite a fancy idea, often seen in the Najdorf Sicilian
We don't play h6 often in the presented lines as the engine has shown that there are some wierd set-ups where white can play only moves and get position with some edge. However, for shorter time controls and for unprepared opponents or against some weird sidelines, various ideas with h6 work like a charm.
I believe there are three good lines here and one has practical value. The absolute best objectively is 7... a6 and this is my recommendation. The two other sound main alternatives are 7... Be7 and 7... Qb6 (the latter is especially popular nowadays and its quite low on theory). Move 7... h6 is a nice move for shorter time controls or if we really need to win, so it deserves honourable mention (albeit, its more on a dubious side)
The move 7... Be7 is perhaps more natural to most of you and resulting positions could be easier to play for lower rated players. My only issue is that there is one concrete line which just go into endgames, which is equal (like maybe +0.3 at worst), but are maybe a bit pleasant for white (white gets activity, but he has a long term weakness). However, if you don't mind it (mind you, even in this endgame there are games at master level where black won) then I think 7... Be7 is just a great line for you, especially at lower levels. Same reasoning goes for 7... Qb6, there is just one line which goes for the endgame were I think its quite easy to play for a win (albeit, we can avoid these lines if we need to, but they are a bit dubious although playable)
In terms of objective evaluation, the 7... a6 is perhaps a bit better, but both are playable (doing analysis on chessify, the 7... a6 line gives about +0.3, maybe +0.4 for white and at the same time 7... Be7 and 7... Qb6 are about +0.4 to +0.5, but the engine has a lot lower confidence there, around 80% which is a rough tell that engine isn't sure what to make of it, especially in 7... Be7 lines) } { [%csl Bg5,Gh6][%cal Bd2g5,Gh7h6] } (7. Qd3!? { New engine's idea. Point is to prepare Qg3 manouver, but here I think we get an easy game } { [%csl Bd5,Gc1,Gd6][%cal Bd3g3,Bg3d6,Ge1c1,Ga1d1,Gd1d6] } 7... Bd7!? { Idea is that we really want to take with the bishop so we have pressure on e4 and therefore, its not easy to play Qg3 due to the fact that the e4 pawn is weak.
In the mainline, we play a6 as we need to prevent Nbd5. Why we don't care about it here? } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bd7c6] } 8. Ndb5 { So, what now? } (8. O-O-O { Mainline, the most natural move. Now Nbd6 is a bit of a threat as we don't have Nb4 } 8... a6 { We stop the before mentioned threat and prepare some b5 bishop in classical sicilian style. Its also another idea behind Bd7, to protect this knight in case of b6 } { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gd4b5,Ga6b5,Bb7b5] } 9. f4 (9. Qg3! { For some reason, its not the most common but seems like the most logical move as Qd3 literally prepares this move. Now, the idea is to put as much pressure on d6 pawn and g7 pawn. I don't think we are in any danger here to be honest. There is one tricky idea here, that I think we should note. Now, any e5! is a lot stronger due to an x-ray on our queen. Therefore, it seems logical to stop any tricks with this tactical motif } { [%csl Gg7,Bd6,Bd8][%cal Gg3g7,Bg3d6,Bd1d8] } 9... Qc7! { We protect d6 and sidestep all e5 stuff. } 10. f4 { White goes for it with idea to play f5 or e5. We have many ways to play this but to me, the queen on g3 seems a bit off now and we can start an attack of our own. But for now, I think we should stick to simplicity } 10... b5! 11. f5 { Best move. Here, there is a nice idea which we should know about. Its quite common reroute in classical sicilian } 11... Nxd4 12. Rxd4 Qc5! $10 { A nice idea to keep in mind, to play Qc5-e5 to propose a queen trade. Then, if they refuse (as if they take we go into good sicilian endgame) we play Be7, O-O with good chances on the queen's side } { [%csl Ge5,Ge7,Gg8][%cal Gc5e5,Gf8e7,Ge8g8] }) 9... Be7! { Now, if they play 10. Nf3 or something along these lines, we will get more or less the mainline, but for some reason white's queen is on d3 instead of d2. Now, there is a reason we do not go for quick b5. The point is that typical Nf3 with intention of e5 is just not as good due to the queen being on d3 rather than d2. } 10. Be2 { Most common move in lichess 2000+ database } (10. Nf3 { This is a standard idea to prepare e5, but here there is a move which is rather tricky if white has a queen on d2 } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge4e5] } 10... Qa5! $10 { Here, we prevent any e5 stuff, b5-b4 is supported and combined with Rc8, we have substantial amount of pressure. Also the reason we don't play this move is that with queen on d2, there are some tricks with sacrificing on e5 or even Nd5!?. Here, none of these work, but we should understand why. } { [%csl Bb4,Ge5,Bc8][%cal Bb7b5,Bb5b4,Ga5e5,Ge4e5,Gd6e5,Ba8c8] } 11. e5?! { Here, we have one and only move. } 11... Nb4! { And this is a reason. Note that 12. Qd2? runs into 12... Nxa2! as 13. Nxa2 Qxa2 and we have a threat of Qa1# } { [%csl Ga2][%cal Gb4a2] } 12. Qd4 dxe5 13. fxe5 Bc5! $17 { Next, we play Nfd5 and we are all set for a queen's side attack. This is the reason e5 is not an option here, but be aware that it could be a move in the future for white. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gf6d5] }) 10... h6! { Useful move as they either give away their bishop pair or they play Bh4 and then their bishop is undefended, which can be quite good as in certain positions we may have tricks with Nxe4 (which is a common tactic in Classical Sicilian)
Now, if they go back to h4, we can just follow with simple Rc8 and b5-b4 in typical Sicilian fashion. However, there is one idea in classical sicilian, which I think you should know. It's to give away a pawn for the open position, sicilian endgame and the bishop pair.
However, we can also play with just Rc8 and b5-b4 without h6. } { [%csl Bg5,Gc8,Gb4][%cal Ga8c8,Gb7b5,Gb5b4] } 11. Bxf6 Bxf6 { 1... gxf6 is maybe playable, but dubious as h6 is then kinda waste of a tempo. Also, at the end of the day, we want to have a compact pawn structure, instead of divided into pawn island structure. } 12. Nxc6?! { But the question is, doesn't this just hang the d6 pawn? } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bd3d6] } 12... Bxc6 13. Qxd6 Qxd6 14. Rxd6 $44 { And this is the position I talked about. Despite being a pawn down, we have the bishop pair, we have ideas to open up some lines for with e.g. Rg8 and g5!, we have centralized king and despite being a pawn down, this position is really good for us. Even if we don't follow up with the most obvious idea, we just have enough compensation for the pawn. } { [%csl Bf6,Bc6,Ge7,Gg8,Gg5][%cal Ge8e7,Gh8g8,Gg7g5] } 14... Bxc3! $15 { And this is the idea. The e4 pawn is weak in many lines and this is an example. Next, we can take on e4. } { [%csl Be4,Ge7][%cal Bc6e4,Ge8e7] }) 8... Nb4 { We use the fact that the queen is on d3 and we can gain a tempo. Now, white must defend the c2 pawn, therefore they cannot play Nxd6+. Now, the main move is Qd1, but the our move is the same as against Qd2 and I think its a lot more natural to play Qd2 in order to still have O-O-O. However, Qd1 gives white one resource they do not have after Qd2 } { [%csl Bd6,Gd3,Bc2][%cal Bf8d6,Gb4d3,Bb4c2] } 9. Qd1 { The mainmove here, which scores extremely well is 7... Qb8 with idea 8. Bf4 e5! going for a Sveshnikov type position. However, engines have showed a nice line which is quite easy, works against both Qd2 and Qd1 and is perhaps even better. However, if you need a dynamic alternative 7... Qb8 will be your friend } 9... Qb8! { This line is quite good for us. We defend d6 and we intend to play a6 with typical Classical sicilian play. Now, white's whole idea is that they want to force us into playing e5 } 10. Bf4 { The whole point, now we are more or less forced into playing e5 (albeit, Bxb5+ is an interesting alternative) } 10... e5 11. Bg5 a6 12. Na3 b5! { Now, we got ourselves a sveshnikov, but with Qb6 and Bd7, which to me seems quiet sensible. } { [%csl Ge7,Gg8][%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8] } 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Nab1 Bg7! $10 { Yoo, Christopher Woojin - Maghsoodloo, Parham, 0-1, 6th Sharjah Masters 2023, https://lichess.org/N8VwPIcB
Here, we can end the line with note that we often play Nc6 (and even Nd4) and f5 to undermine white's centre. To me, it looks like a really good Sveshnikov } { [%csl Gf5,Gd4][%cal Gf6f5,Gb4c6,Gc6d4] }) (7. Bb5 { A sideline, white's idea is to give away their light squared bishop (which is often quite useless). I don't think its an ambitious set-up, but it makes sense from white's perspective. However, we will get a lot easier to play endgames if they give away their bishop pair. Sicilian is itself known for good endgames for black, so if you add to it the bishop pair, I don't think we should worry } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bd4c6,Bb5c6] } 7... Bd7 { Most natural, we unpin and protect the knight } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gd7c6] } 8. Qd3 { Here, we have many possible continuations. The simplest one is 8... Qb6 forcing some trades. Another line is 8... h6 with ideas to even play g5 at some point. We can also continue with modest 8... Be7 or 8... Ne5. I will recommend on of these moves, but just know that there are many ideas for black.
Out of these moves, the most fighting one is definitely 8... h6 } 8... Qb6! { This is by far the simplest lines of all and we get a simple endgame to play. I don't think we should go for some really deep lines against a sideline you will rarely see. } 9. O-O-O Nxd4 { And we just trade down to the endga,e } 10. Qxd4 Qxd4 11. Rxd4 a6! { This move is really important, even if they take our bishop. The point is we stop Nb5! ideas. If they go back, we just finish our development } { [%csl Ba6,Bc7,Bd6,Gc2,Ge7][%cal Bc3b5,Bb5c7,Bb5d6,Ga8c8,Gc8c2,Gf8e7] } 12. Bxd7+ Nxd7 13. Rhd1 Rc8! { We put or rook on semi-open line. Our next plan is to kick that bishop, so h4-h5 with idea Ra5! is a nice idea. If they play h4 themselves, then f6 is a good idea. } { [%csl Gc2,Bh4,Bf6,Ge5,Gh5][%cal Gc8c2,Bh7h5,Bh5h4,Bf7f6,Gd7e5,Gh8h5] } 14. Bh4 { Blocking our h-pawn and preparing Bg3 } { [%csl Gh4,Gd6][%cal Gh4g3,Gg3d6] } 14... h5! $10 { We still play h4 so that they cannot play 15. Bg3?? e5! 16. R4d2 h4! traps the bishop. Also, we have ideas of g5! Bxg5 Rg8 or just Ne5 to centralize our knight (then, f6 is a good move too so that this knight is actually well defended and then we can go for quick g5! ideas. Position is balanced, but the imbalance in the pawn structure makes this position not that drawish, at least at club level. } { [%csl Bh4,Gg5,Gg2,Ge5][%cal Bh5h4,Gg7g5,Gh8g8,Gg8g2,Gd7e5] }) (7. f4?! { This is second most popular move on club level. The idea is that white plays for some sort of Bg5-type Najdorf type position. Here however, its just a nothing burger. } 7... h6! { This is a quite nice move. The idea is that white either takes on f6 and then we take with our queen, we have the bishop pair and nothing to worry about or they play Bh4 and their bishop is no longer protected so there are ideas of Nxe4!! in certain lines. } 8. Bh4 { 8. Bxf6 Qxf6 is nothing so I won't even analyze it, we get easy game here } 8... Be7 { And now, white must be careful as e.g. 9. Qd2? runs into 9... Nxe4!! and the fact that the bishop on h4 is not protected proves quite important. Now, the most typical move is Qf3 (in analogous variation in Najdorf), but now most people realize that this just hangs the knight. Therefore, they trade first } { [%csl Bh4] } 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Qf3 { But this, is just not good as we can quite easily strike in the centre } 10... d5! 11. O-O-O Rb8! $15 { We set up some tactics on b7 pawn. Next, we play Qb6 or Qa5, the e4 pawn is weak and position seems quite good for us. We wait with O-O as we want to generate enough counterplay on the queen's side so that white's attack with g4-g5 doesn't land first. Also, without O-O, our king defends our bishop which could be important especially considering the fact that white has option of playing e5. } { [%csl Bb2,Ga3,Ba5,Rg5,Be7][%cal Bd8b6,Bb8b2,Ga7a5,Ga5a4,Ga4a3,Bb6a5,Rg2g4,Rg4g5] }) 7... a6 { Really important move. It prepares b5 and also prevents any Nbd5 ideas (if we were to play 7... Bd7? then 8. Nbd5! would be problematic. This is the SuperGM mainline nowadays. } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bd4b5] } 8. O-O-O { Here, Black has at least two decent moves. The classical mainline with to just play 9... Bd7 and then to continue with 10... b5. However, these lines can get rather tricky to play as we play with a king in the centre and for a club player, it can be hard (for some, it can be quite easy and fun on the other hand). For that reason, there is a more modern try of 10... Be7 and to play then 11... b5. Now, in recent years many top grandmasters had been playing this 9... Be7 10. f3!? move with idea to play 11. h4 and continue in English style attack. Black scores decently in these lines, but players on the black side discovered that you can start with 9... Be7 and then after 10. f3 we can play 10... Nxd4!? 11. Qxd4 b5! with idea that we now can play 12... Bb7 and our bishop is much better placed on b7 as it now supports d5 break. The move 9... Be7 is quite well known as it was the classical way of playing the Richter Rauzer. Black wanted to then play 10... O-O and 11... Qa5! with a king's side attack. I think these lines are quite fun for black and to me, the only problem with it is that white can go for equalish endgame with quick e5 push in certain lines. Leaving that aside, here we have to main options:
- To start with 8... Bd7 and then play 9... Be7
- To start with 8... Be7 and then play 9... Bd7
You may wonder, why we would play 8... Bd7 in light of the fact that it just allows improved 9. f3. Well, the reason is that white can trick us with many move orders like 9. h4!? and then we must either transpose with 9... Bd7 or make a waiting move like 10... Qc7. Also, white gains one strong reply in this line, namely 9. Bxf6! as then we don't have 9... Qxf6 nor 9... Bxf6? as then 10. Nxc6! and the d6 pawn hangs. I think that both moves are fine and its mostly matter of taste. The main line of 10. f4 transposes into each other. With move 8... Be7 we can go into the classical mainline stuff, but for the purpose of this repertoire I won't delve much into these variations (however, you can look into one of the alternatives which goes for this Be7 and O-O plan, albeit we don't play a6 first for certain reasons). For the purpose of this repertoire, we will go for 8...Bd7 as I think it is quite good for club level, the 9. f3 its quite hard to play for white if we know one little idea and I think, for club level player, this is a lot more manageable then knowing all the possible side variations that white can throw at us.
I want to mention that these two moves are not the only two playable moves. There is also 9... Nxd4 which is also an interesting alternative and 9... h6!? which was quite popular before the engines. I would love to recommend this move but in recent years engines have found a weird set-up which is quite problematic for this line. However, if you are facing an unprepared opponent, someone a lot weaker or just short time control game, this can be a quite nice line to play. } { [%csl Bd7,Ge7][%cal Bc8d7,Gf8e7] } (8. Nxc6 { Thi s is a minor try, where I think white just has nothing. The point is that our next move is Bd7 and now, we don't have option of Bxc6. However, I don't think much of these lines } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bc8d7,Bd7c6] } 8... bxc6 9. O-O-O Be7 { Next, we can kick the bishop, finish our development with Bb7 and play for d5 as we want to open up the position for our bishop pair (and we want to grab our fair share of centre) } { [%csl Bh6,Gb7,Gd5][%cal Bh7h6,Gc8b7,Gd6d5] } 10. Bxf6 { Most played move. Now we must weaken our pawn structure because d6 pawn is weak } { [%csl Bd6] } 10... gxf6 11. Bc4 Bb7! { Here, we aim to finish our development and play d5 at some point. Our main idea to develop our queen and then play O-O-O with decent play, but more on that later. } { [%csl Ba5,Gc7][%cal Bd8a5,Gd8c7] } 12. Bb3 Qc7 { [%csl Bc8][%cal Be8c8] } 13. Rhe1 O-O-O! $10 { And here, we can end the line. Next, we will slowly improve our position with e.g. Kb8, maybe Qa5, maybe h5-h4-h3 (this move also prevents any Qh6-h5 ideas, which target our f7 pawn. This is quite common theme in these lines that white wants to get this on the board), maybe Rg8 etc. Then, if we have nothing to improve we strike with d5. } { [%csl Bb8,Gc8,Gd5,Gg2,Bh3,Rh6][%cal Bc8b8,Gd8c8,Gd6d5,Gh8g8,Gg8g2,Bh7h5,Bh5h4,Bh4h3] }) 8... Bd7! { We would love to play 8... h6 and then to put out bishop on b7, however this runs into 9. Nxc6! bxc6 10. Bf4 line and white can get some small edge and therefore, I don't want to give this as my main recommendation because many people just brute-force chessable courses (I had situations were people asked my during online chess games if I have bought a certain course as I play the best line from that course). Therefore, we don't do it here.
Now, our main idea with Bd7 is that we now can take on c6 with Nxc6. Also, we are one move quicker with O-O-O (something quite common in this variation). The main darwback of this move is that Bb7 is not really a viable plan now. However, in many lines, I think bishop on d7 is just easier to play as we can play b5-b4 and white cannot play Na4, which is often annoying
Now, the way we play is almost entirely dependent on whether white goes for f3 or f4. If they play with f4 (intending Nf3 and e5) we strike quickly with b5. If they play for more positional f3, then we have other options, bust mostly centered around first making sure there is no attack on the king's side (with h5!?) and then playing Ne5 and b5-b4. We will touch on that more later } { [%csl Bc6,Bh6,Ga4,Gf2][%cal Bd7c6,Bh7h6] } 9. f4 { Here, we have two (well, three) main options. The old classical line is 9... b5 and the modern line is 9... Be7 (some people even play 8... Be7 but then 9. Bxf6 is a bit annoying, but the idea is that 9. f3 lines are a lot worse. However, to me it just looks like more theory as white can more or less tranpose with 9. Kb1 into 9. f3 "improved" lines). Also, it seems like 9... Be7 is perhaps less sharp and allows us to be a bit more lazy. Its mostly matter of taste and I will give you both moves. However, my main recommendation will be 9... Be7 as it seems its easier to make work for club players with limited time for study. There is also rather dubious, but extremely rewarding especially against weaker and unprepared opponents variations 9... h6 with idea that 10. Bh4 (10. Bxf6?! Qxf6 gives us bishop pair for nothing) runs into either 10... Nxe4!! or 10... g5!?. Engine isn't fan of either of those, but practically they are quite good and black scores well there. I really like this lines especially for blitz.
Also, fun fact, in recent years at master level, 9... Be7 scores better for black than for white, so just now that this is quite sound for black. } (9. f3 { This is I think the mainline nowadays at the top level. White more or less wants to play English Attack. Grandmasters have their reasons they want to play this, but to me, it seems like bishop on g5 makes this king's side pawn storm a lot harder to achieve and we have in particular at least one really nice idea against it, which I think will give you some nice wins. } 9... Be7 10. h4 { Here, I will recommend to you the old mainline. However, there are some nice new ideas here e.g. 10... Nxd4!? with idea to play 11. Qxd4 b5! with typical sicilian play. However, I think practically speaking, the line I want to recommend to you will give you nice results } (10. g4?! { Its quite reasonable to start with g4, but from practical standpoint, I think this move is a bit inaccurate. } 10... h5! { This one little move renders white's attack borderline done. The idea is, we will get the knight on h5, which will protect the g7 pawn, there will be no weaknesses on the king's side and we will get typical sicilian queen's side (or even king's side) counterplay } 11. gxh5 Nxh5 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. Rg1 g6! { Now, there is nothing to attack on the king's side } 14. f4 O-O-O! $10 { Here, white has multiple weaknesses, we can play Kb8 and use our c-file and I don't see any target for white to attack here. To me, practically speaking, black has small edge here. } { [%csl Gb8,Gc8,Bf4,Be4,Bh2][%cal Gc8b8,Gd8c8] }) 10... h6! { A nice move as now, either white gives up their bishop pair or they must go back } 11. Be3 { Well, now you may ask: "Why we force white to improve their bishop? Now, they can play g4-g5!" - and here, the second part of our plan comes to life } 11... h5!? { We just prevent any g4 idea. Our next idea is to play Ne5 (note that now any f4 runs into Neg4) and then b4-b5 with massive queen's side attack.
Movsesian, S. - Li Chao2, 0-1, IMSA Rapid 2016, lichess.org/lJsoZLDi } { [%csl Bg4,Ge5,Gb4][%cal Bh5g4,Bf6g4,Gc6e5,Gb7b5,Gb5b4] } 12. Nxc6! { This quite directly prevents our Ne5 plan, so I think we should take a look at it } (12. Bg5! { Mainline. I mean, white wants to just improve their bishop as on g5, it stands nicely and white has no hope for g4-g5 anyways } 12... Ne5! { We continue with our plan of Ne5 and b5-b4 } 13. Kb1 b5 { We just go for it. Now we have ideas of Nc4 and we want to meet 14. f4 with Neg4! as then, the king's side is blocked and white cannot attack us over there. } { [%csl Bc4][%cal Be5c4] } 14. a3 Rb8! $15 { And we then push with Qb6 and a5 with b4. Of course, white can play this position a bit better, but we went the most played continuation, assuming white knows quite "weird" 12. Bg5. This just shows you how hard this position is for white. I played a lot of Taimanov Sicilian before Classical and I must say, this is a dream position against English Attack. } { [%csl Bb6,Bb4,Ba5][%cal Bd8b6,Ba6a5,Bb5b4] }) 12... Bxc6! { Here, our idea is quite simple - we want to push d5. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd6d5,Gc6d5,Gf6d5] } 13. Kb1 Qc7! { We sidestep any issues on the d-file and now we are ready with d5 push } { [%csl Bd8,Gd5][%cal Rd1d8,Ba8d8,Gd6d5,Gf6d5,Gc6d5] } 14. Bg5 d5 15. e5 Ng8! $10 { We prepare Nxe7-f5, we then can play with O-O-O and position is about balanced } { [%csl Bf5,Bf6,Gc8][%cal Bg8e7,Be7f5,Gg8h6,Gh6f5,Bf7f6,Ge8c8] }) (9. Kb1 { This is mostly a move order trick. White doesn't show what they want to play yet (namely, if they are going to play f3 or f4). } { [%csl Bf2] } 9... Be7 { We continue as usual. } 10. Nxc6 { Only move with some independent value, but here we once against get a typical Classical sicilian play } (10. f4 b5! { Here, we get improved mainline as white "wasted" a tempo on Kb1 and now, they don't have 11. Nf3 with e5 as 11... b4! is just to quick. } 11. Nxc6 Bxc6 12. Bxf6 { This seems to me to be the only logical continuation, but here we have a nice equalizing trick. If we play 12... gxf6?! then f5! is a bit problematic as white gets improved mainline (as we don't have h5 and Bh6 and we have already traded knights, also we don't have Qb6 yet). However, there is a nice idea which just gives us at least equal endgame } 12... Bxf6! { Seemingly hangs a pawn, but... } 13. Qxd6 Qxd6 14. Rxd6 Rc8! { Now, there is a threat of Bxc3! and then Bxe4 } { [%csl Bc3,Ge4][%cal Bf6c3,Gc6e4] } 15. e5 Be7 16. Rd1 Bc5! $15 { And good luck to white on ever moving. Their only choice is to part ways with g2 pawn and then we just have the bishop pair. It is often the case that taking on d6 for white is just not good. } { [%csl Gg8,Bh1,Bg1][%cal Ge8g8,Bc6h1,Bc5g1] }) (10. f3 { This will most likely tranpose into the 9. f3 modern mainline. } 10... Qc7! { General improving move. Now, b5 is an idea. Also, we have our typical h5 here, so e.g. 11. g4 runs into 11... h5 (or, we can go for an improvement with 11... Nxd4 12. Qxd4 h5! } { [%csl Bb5,Gh5][%cal Bb7b5,Gh7h5] } 11. Be3 h5! $10 { To showcase something new. We just prevent g4 and we play chess. Next we go for typical Ne5 and b5-b4 plan with marvelous position } { [%csl Be5,Gb4][%cal Bc6e5,Gb7b5,Gb5b4] }) 10... Bxc6! { And the plan is clear, break with d5 if allowed } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd6d5,Gc6d5,Gf6d5] } 11. f3 h6! { Typical 11... Qc7 is also fine, but to show you another way of playing these positions, we will start with h6 as it is quite useful move in many classical sicilian positions as it unpins our knight, creates a luft and albeit, creates a hook, it doesn't matter often if we can get d5 in. } 12. Be3 { Here, we can start with 12... Qc7 if we are scared of moves like 13. Qf2. However, in that position we have 13... Qb8! with fine game } 12... d5 13. e5 { Best and most natural. Here, we get a French structure. } 13... Nd7 14. f4 Qa5! $10 { Next, we play long castle and we aim for French Type Endgame position. } { [%csl Gc8][%cal Ge8c8] }) 9... b5! { This is the old mainline. Nice thing about this particular variation is that at club level, basically in half of the games here, white played at least an inaccuracy. That's why I like this like particularly for club players - our ideas are quite simple, white has maybe 3 variations we must know and that's pretty much about it.
Our idea is that white signals to us they want to strike with e5 and mate us as fast as they can. Therefore, we start our own attack.
I want to stress, that it could be the case that 9... Be7 is a touch better at higher levels, assuming both sides know the theory really well. However, in club level games, it is quite clear that white mostly have no clue what to do in these positions. They either study grandmaster courses and have no clue how to play these lines or they just forget. As a matter of fact, only 30% of games here continue with the best move. That means, we have 70% chance they play something suboptimal. However, in 9... Be7 there is higher chances of them playing correctly. I think the practical value of this move supersedes the theoretical evaluation, which is still equal albeit we must know one concrete variation. That being said, 9... Be7 is a great alternative and if you need an alternative, feel free to analyze it. } { [%csl Bf5,Bh6,Gb4][%cal Bf4f5,Bd2h6,Gb5b4] } 10. Bxf6 { Best move, white forces us to weaken our structure due to 10... Qxf6? 11. e5! and as we can see, the bishop on d7 is hanging in many lines } (10. Nxc6 { Really common continuation, which gives us fine play } 10... Bxc6 { Now, if allowed we will just go for Be7, O-O and typical sicilian onslaught. To see how to play these positions, check the 10. a3 line. However, the most common move here is just a mistake. Close to 60% of all games in lichess 2000+ blitz or slower database continues with a mistake, so therefore I will mostly analyze this variation.
Note that if white tries to tranpose into the mainline (10. Bxf6) with 11. Bxf6 then 11... Qxf5 and there are no tactics with 12. e5 as there is no Nbxd5 tactics as there is no knight to take after axb5. } { [%csl Be7,Bg8,Gc7][%cal Bf8e7,Be8g8,Gd8c7] } 11. e5? { This just gives us a really good endgame where in lichess 2000+ blitz or slower database, black scores more than 70% here } (11. Bd3 { This has sometimes been tried at the top level, but I don't think we should worry about it. General rule of thumb is that if white doesn't go for the cutting edge mainline, then we should play the most normal chess imaginable. } 11... Be7! $10 { And we want to just castle with a fine position. We can include h6. It is quite common theme, but again its mostly just the fact that if white takes on f6, we get the bishop pair, if they retreat, we have tactical ideas of Nxe4!?. Also, if white plays e.g. 12. Rhe1 setting up Nd5 ideas, then quick 12... O-O is a good idea, just to prevent all these threats. And then, we have quite simple idea of attacking on the queen's side with b4. } { [%csl Gg8,Bh6][%cal Ge8g8,Bh7h6] }) 11... dxe5 { Now, we entre quite forcing line. If white plays e.g. 12. Qe1 then simple 12... Qc7 is fine, there is nothing special about it } { [%csl Bc7][%cal Bd8c7] } 12. Qxd8+ Rxd8 13. Rxd8+ Kxd8 14. fxe5 { We seemingly lose a piece } 14... h6!! $19 { But we don't! Its a common trick in many openings. Here, in lichess 2000+ blitz or slower database this position occurred around 4000 times and black scores here staggering 77%. The reason is, white has weak e5 pawn and this is dream sicilian endgame with the bishop pair, nice minority attack and active king in the centre. } { [%csl Bg5,Bb4,Ba5,Ge5][%cal Bg7g5,Ba6a5,Bb5b4] }) (10. a3 { Common and not that dumb move. Here, we will just go for the most typical sicilian Rauzer. White stoped b4 (for now) but in doing so, he created a hook so going for typical, old line Rauzer with Be7 and O-O makes a lot of sense as now, our attack comes a lot quicker } 10... Be7 { Now, a nice reply to any Nb3 or Nf3 preparing e5 then we have a strong reply of b4! e.g. 11. Nf3 b4 (also 11... Qa5 is also interesting) 12. axb4 Nxb4! and 13. e5? runs into 13... Qa5!. } { [%csl Gg8,Bb4,Bd5][%cal Ge8g8,Bb5b4,Bc6b4,Bf6d5,Bb4d5] } 11. Be2 { Most popular move and quite logical. White sets up g4 and Bf3 moreover, this move connects the rooks. } 11... O-O! $10 { And our main idea now is to play b4! so that after e5 we have (at the very least) Nfd5!. We also can go for moves like Qc7, Rfd8 or Qb6. Generally, its just sicilian mess where we try to mate them on the queen's side and they try to mate us on the king's side. The main of our counterplay is b4! so keep that in mind } { [%csl Bb4,Gc7,Gd8][%cal Bb5b4,Gd8c7,Gf8d8] }) (10. Qe1!? { This is quite interesting move. White sets up both b4 and Nd5 and if we are not accurate, he may get something. However, white's entire idea is that they can get Nd5, so we will make sure they cannot with a rather simple idea. Note this plan as it is quite useful if you play something you've never seen as it takes a lot of tactical venom out of position } { [%csl Ge5,Bd5][%cal Ge4e5,Bc3d5] } 10... Nxd4 11. Rxd4 Qb6! { We take on d4 to gain tempo and prepare Bc6 so that Nd5 is not an issue } { [%csl Bc6][%cal Bd7c6] } 12. Rd2 b4! { Now, we continue with Be7, Bc6 (prepares d5 and Nd7 if needed), perhaps h6, Rc8 and we get a game of chess. White's only idea is e5 so make sure there are not tactical issues for us after this move. Except that, white must worry about the fact that we will play d5 at some point and can pursue simple minority attack on the queen's side. Also, I wouldn't rush with O-O as the king on e8 nicely protects the bishop on e7 so that e5 isn't an issue. } { [%csl Bd8,Ge7,Gg8,Bc6,Re5,Be8][%cal Be8d8,Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Bd7c6,Re4e5] }) 10... gxf6 11. Kb1 { Here, white can start with 11. Nxc6 or 11. f5 but this will most likely tranpose so its pointless to analyze each independently. I only show 11. f5 as it has some instructional value } (11. f5 { This is a sometimes played move order. This will most likely tranpose into our mainline } 11... Qb6 { Same as in the mainline. We put pressure on the d4 knight } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc6d4,Gb6d4] } (11... Nxd4!? { Here, I want to show you one important idea which is quite common in many Rauzer Structures } 12. Qxd4 Bh6+! { Our idea - Bh6-f4-e5 reroute. This is also why white likes Kb1 move, Bh6+ doesn't come with check. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bh6f4,Bf4e5] } 13. Kb1 Bf4 14. fxe6 fxe6 15. Ne2 Be5! $13 { This is the resulting position. Our bishop is a monster on e5. Next, Qc7-c5 is an idea and Ke7. This plan of putting our bishop on e5 is common especially if white doesn't play Kb1, so keep that in mind. Now, we can play both on the king's side and on the queen's side } { [%csl Be5,Bc5,Ge7][%cal Bd8c7,Bc7c5,Ge8e7] }) 12. Nxc6 Bxc6 { Now, 13. Kb1 Qc5 tranposes into the mainline. } 13. fxe6 fxe6 { Here again, 14. Kb1 Qc5 tranposes into the mainline } 14. Bd3 { Sometimes played, but here, we will show white why the move Kb1 is quite useful in these lines } 14... b4 { Our typical move in these positions, its always useful. } 15. Ne2 h5! { And now white must deal with Bh6 threat } { [%csl Bc1][%cal Bf8h6,Bh6c1] } 16. Kb1 Bh6 17. Qe1 Qc5! $10 { And here we got pretty much same position as in the mainline. I annotated this part quite sparsely, because its quite clear there is pretty much no independent value in quicker f5 push. }) (11. Nxc6 Bxc6 12. Qe1!? { Interesting line where white tries to get Nd5 in. However, if we see this threat and stop it we will get the most standard Rauzer Position } { [%csl Bd5,Re8][%cal Bc3d5,Re1e8] } 12... Be7 { We prevent any Nd5 as now, there is no check and we have option of Kf8 } 13. Bd3 Qb6 14. Kb1 b4! $10 { Here, we get typical Rauzer position, we may continue with d5, O-O-O and a5. } { [%csl Bd5,Ga5,Gc8][%cal Bd6d5,Ga6a5,Ge8c8] }) 11... Qb6! { The main purpose of this move is to "threaten" the endgame where we have the bishop pair, two open files and massive blob of pawns in the centre } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4,Bb6d4] } 12. Nxc6 (12. Nce2 { This is an alternative to 12. Nxc6, albeit I don't think this its as critical as the before mentioned move as we often play b4 in order to induce Nce2. so I think this is just a nice option for us. } 12... h5! { I like to include this move as we still can play O-O-O, we still allow white to go for Nxc6 into lines well known to us and its is generally just a useful move } { [%csl Bh6][%cal Bf8h6] } 13. h4 { White fixes our h5 pawn and intends to attack it. Now, The move I will recommend gives black white option to enter about equal endgame. If you need more as you are in a must-win game, consider playing here 13... Bh6 as then any 14. Ng3?? just doesn't work as the f4 pawn is hanging. } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Be2g3,Bg3h5] } (13. g3 { This is a general useful move in these structures as white wants often to play Bh3 and put presure on e6 with f5!. However, here white takes away the possibility of Ng3 which is often quite important if white wants to have some counterplay on the king's side } { [%csl Rg3][%cal Re2g3] } 13... Na5 { [%csl Gc4][%cal Ga5c4] } 14. b3 Rb8!? { This sets up threats of Nc4!, a quite common trick. Then, d5! opening up our dark squared bishop and white is in some trouble. The main issue is that there are mates on b2, so a nice prophylactic move is 15.c3 but then 15... b4! gives us nice play as 16. c4 runs into 16... Nb7 with Nc5 in mind and we are doing quite well. } 15. Bg2 Nc4!! $15 { The idea I just mentioned. Now, there is a really nice idea. Naturally, 16. Qd3 Na3+ is fine for us, with d5 and e5 incoming. But there is more vicious idea. Against 16. Qc1 we have 16... e5! 17. Nf5 d5!! and now, after 18. bxc4 bxc4+ 19. Ka1 d4! we get massive centre, bishop pair and I have no idea how white defends as Qa5 and Ba3 is a huge threat. This is nice idea to remember. Of course, you make sure everything works, but its a nice thing to know.
On the side node, this Na3+ is a nice idea even if the pawn is on b2 and we have our pawn on b4, as then the same theme (white takes our knight and we retake with the b-pawn opening up lines for our heavy pieces) is a common theme in classical sicilian, so keep that in mind. } { [%csl Be5,Ga5,Ga3,Gd5][%cal Be6e5,Ga6a5,Gc4a3,Gd6d5] }) (13. Qd3 { a new wierd move. I wanted to analyze it purely cause there is an interesting idea which I think may be quite useful to know } 13... Ne7!? { We want to play d5! here. Also, we often play Bg7 with idea to play f5 and open up that bishop. This move seems weird but its the mainline and I think you may find this idea quite useful and you could implement it in other, similar positions } { [%csl Bd5,Gb4,Ga5,Gg7,Gd8,Bc8][%cal Bd6d5,Ga6a5,Gb5b4,Gf8g7,Ga8d8,Ba8c8] } 14. g3 Rd8 { We set-up some tactical ideas against white's queen. This also deters the e5 move. } { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gd8d3] } 15. Bg2 Bg7! $10 { Next, we may play f5, maybe a5 with b4 and we get a nice game. We can open up the board for our bishops, we can expand on the queen's side and white has no pawn breaks. } { [%csl Gd5,Bf5,Bb4,Ba5][%cal Gd6d5,Bf6f5,Bb5b4,Ba6a5] }) 13... b4! { We take away the c3 square for good. Now, e5 moves are more likely to work. There are other moves which work, but this is a nice idea I wanted to show you. Another good move is Bh6 or Rd8 which is just prophylaxis. } { [%csl Bb4,Bc3,Bh6,Gd8][%cal Be2c3,Bf8h6,Ga8d8] } 14. Ng3 { I think it is important to show why this move just doesn't win a pawn } { [%csl Bh5][%cal Bf1e2,Be2h5,Bg3h5] } (14. Rh3!? { With intention to triple up on the d-file. Quite interesting rook lift, albeit I don't think it accomplishes much } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bh3d3] } 14... a5 { We defend b4. Now, we have many ideas in these positions. One of main ideas is to strike with either e5 or d5. Also, O-O-O is common move to prepare before mentioned breaks. Also, a4 preparing b3 and Na5-c4 is good. So is Rc8 in many positions. } { [%csl Bc8,Gb4,Ge5,Ba4,Bc4][%cal Be8c8,Ge6e5,Ba5a4,Bc6a5,Ba5c4,Ga8c8] } 15. c4! { Interesting move, with idea to play Nb5. } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bd4b5] } 15... Nxd4 16. Nxd4 Be7! $10 { Now 17. Nb5 Bc6 seems fine for us as d6 pawn is well protected (as 18. Nxd6? runs into 18... Bxd6 19. Qxd6 Bxe4!), Next, Rd8 to make sure the d6 pawn is well protected and then Qb7 with idea to play d5 is a good way to continue the game. } { [%csl Ge4,Bf8,Bb8,Gd5,Bd8][%cal Gd7c6,Gc6e4,Be8f8,Ba8b8,Gb6b7,Gb7d5,Gd6d5,Ba8d8] }) 14... Nxd4! { We will go for a fine endgame for us. If you need to win, you can play an alternative 14... Be7. } 15. Qxd4 Qxd4 16. Rxd4 a5! { We defend unprotected b4 pawn } { [%csl Gb4][%cal Ga5b4] } 17. Be2 Rg8! $10 { And we will get the our pawn back or we will get a lot of activity for it. Its equal and there is still some imbalance to play for. } { [%csl Gg2][%cal Gg8g2] }) 12... Bxc6 13. f5 { The critical try. Here, we have many moves and honestly, if white wants to go for this they must know a lot of theory. I will recommend to you a new move, which to me makes a lot of sense. The line I want to recommend to you is objectively equal and best white can get is a draw. However, there are many other tries here. The recommended line has one small issue - white has one promising line which goes quite deep. On the good side, if we do not remember the exact move, then we will get just a really complicated position. However, we need to know one quite long (24 moves deep) variation.
Interesting alternative is 13... O-O-O!?. Black scores really were here and it seems like its harder to mis some tactical shot than in other lines. The old mainline (which is objectively equal too) is 13... b4 and its quite fine too, but it can get a bit to drawish for my liking. There is also 13... h5!? which is quite interesting as it prepares quick Bh6 move. All of these moves are good and playable (and there are other decent and probably playable moves too (e.g. 13... Rg8). If you want to prep something for a given opponent here, its quite easy I must say } (13. Qe1!? { White sets up Nd5 tactics } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc3d5] } 13... O-O-O! { We just step aside all the tactical issues with long castle. Also, note that 14. f5?! runs into 14... b4! 15. Ne2 e5! and white cannot use the d5 square. } 14. Bd3 b4! { Standard response, we want to kick that knight back to take control over the d5 square } { [%csl Bd5] } 15. Ne2 d5! $10 { Trying to open up some lines for our bishop and to put some pressure on the queen's side.. White can take, which plays into our hand, white can play 16. e5 but then 16... f5! and good luck for white even creating any counterplay against our king. White can also wait with 16. Qh4 with idea that after 16... Be7?! 17. e5 is a bit problematic, but then we can play Bc5 (so that the rooks protect themselves so we have dxe4) and we chill.
Berescu, Alin Mile - Kozul, Zdenko, 0-1, Djakovo op-A, 2005, https://lichess.org/qeKgHL0t } { [%csl Ba2][%cal Bc6d5,Bd5a2] }) 13... Qc5! { Idea is to have Qe5 whenever we need to defend our pawns and we want to threaten the queen trade (as in many cases, Qe5 will offer such queen trade). Now, our idea is to play b4 and h5 with Bh6, put our king in the centre and vibe to this insane position. One thing you must know is that you should be really careful about the move e5.
Be careful about Nd5 ideas especially if you put your queen on e5.
Typical idea is to play for b4 with a5-a4-a3
Nice feature of this position is that white must be extremely accurate to not be worse here. } { [%csl Bb4,Gh5,Gh6,Be5,Re8,Rd5,Ba3][%cal Bb5b4,Gh7h5,Gf8h6,Bc5e5,Rh1e1,Re1e8,Rc3d5,Ba6a5,Ba5a4,Ba4a3] } 14. Bd3 { Here, we have two moves which gives us fine game. Both have their subtleties. The moves are 14... b5 and 14... h5. Now, both equalize and both of them have an idea behind them. But I think its a lot harder to play as white against one than the other. On the other hand, if white finds all 10 top engine moves, position is extremely unclear and in the other, we get equal endgame. } { [%csl Bb4,Bh5][%cal Bb5b4,Bh7h5] } 14... h5! { My preferred move. Idea is quite simple, we just want to play Bh6. Now, to me it looks like if we can put a queen on e5, defending both weak f6 and d6 and we put our bishop on h6 (and then we can even think about Bf4-e5), this position looks quite good for black.
If allowed and white plays a random move e.g. 15. Rhf1? then we can grab a better position with 15... b4! 16. Ne2 Bh6 17. Qe1 e5! and the centre is closed therefore our king is safe, we have the bishop pair, nice queen's side initiative and white has nothing really. } { [%csl Bh6,Ge5,Bb4][%cal Bf8h6,Gc5e5,Bb5b4] } 15. fxe6 $132 { Only move which tries to get something. But its a double edged move. White opens up position and leaves himself with weak e4 pawn. at the same time, we have the bishop pair.
On the side note, white can play first 14. fxe6 fxe6 15. Bd3. But this position is an overwhelmingly the mainline } { [%csl Bf8,Bc6,Re4] } (15. Qe1 { This is a line, which is played at the top level. Here, we need to know our lines but if we know them, we at least equalize. White wants to use the fact that our king is still in the centre. } { [%csl Be8][%cal Be1e8] } 15... b4 { If white goes back, we continue with 16... e5 and white will never use the d5 square. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge6e5] } 16. Nd5!! { Best move, here it seems like we are in some trouble, but to me it looks like we can force quite a nice late middlegame (or endgame) position. We do not take with the pawn as then we will just be killed down the e-file } 16... Bxd5 17. exd5 e5! $10 { And it looks like to me, we are at least equal. There is no danger to our king, we still can target white's weaknesses on e5 or g2. Our king will be nicely placed on e7 and our only problem is that our bishop on f8 is inactive, but this can change quite quickly as it can be reroute into e.g. b6 square via Be7-d8-b6 idea. Also, we have a protected passed pawn. Now, it doesn't mean much but its a small plus for us. White's main source of couterplay is weak c6 square and weak h5 pawn but it is often the case that taking the pawn on h5 just loses the game for white as we often pin something on h5 or it just loses too much time. } { [%csl Be5,Ga5,Be7,Gg2,Gb6,Rc6,Rh5][%cal Ga6a5,Be8e7,Gh8g8,Gg8g2,Gf8e7,Ge7d8,Gd8b6] }) 15... fxe6 16. Qe1! { Now here, white once again wants to play Nd5, but here, in comparison to 15. Qe1, white has an open f-file too. Now, 16... Qe5 completely equalizes for black. However, I think its a bit theory heavy (we are at move 16... by the way) so I think we should play something else. However, if you are fine with a draw and learning some lines, 16... Qe5 might be a move for you. } { [%csl Bd5,Rf6][%cal Bc3d5,Rh1f1,Rf1f6] } (16. Rhf1 { Other popular move, which gives us equality after quite typical fashion for this line } 16... b4! { We want to make sure there are no Nd5 tricks and we play b4 now. 17. Nd5?! runs into 17... exd5 18. exd5 Bd7 19. Rxf6 O-O-O and our king is safe. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc3d5] } 17. Ne2 Bh6! { The idea behind h5 is now clear } 18. Qe1 Ke7! $10 { Seems like black is doing fine here, we can play with a5 and we have play on both the king's side and the queen's side. If white plays Nf4, then we take it as this knight is often quite strong and there are many tactical ideas with e.g. Nxe6. Also, the h4 pawn would be hanging and Ng6+ would be an issue.
Vuillemin, Gaston - Saglione, Eduardo, 0-1, CLW I  (FRA), 2012, https://lichess.org/916jYdXS } { [%csl Ba5,Rg6,Bf4][%cal Ba6a5,Re2f4,Rf4g6,Bh6f4] }) (16. e5! { Perhaps the most direct approach, but here we are fine } 16... fxe5! { We make sure the d-file is closed as Bg6+ would be quite problematic if not for Kd7. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Be8d7] } 17. Ne4 Bxe4! { We must take as there is no good way to prevent all the treats this knight created } 18. Bxe4 Rc8! { We put the rook on open c-file and intend to finish our king's side development } { [%csl Gc2,Be7][%cal Gc8c2,Bf8e7] } 19. Bb7 { White may think they won a pawn, but they cannot take on a6 } { [%csl Ga6,Gc8][%cal Gb7a6,Gb7c8] } 19... Rb8! $10 { We next play Be7 and Kd7, maybe d5 and position is quite unbalanced for 3 results. We are up a pawn but our king is quite weak. We have nice centre, passed pawns and position seems quite fun to play
Note that 20. Bxa6?? runs into 20... Qb6! } { [%csl Ba6,Bb6,Gf6,Ge7,Bd7][%cal Bb7a6,Bc5b6,Gf8e7,Ge7f6,Ge8e7,Be8d7] }) 16... Bg7!? { This move allows us to play either O-O or O-O-O or to just keep our king in the centre. Moreover, we protect the f6 pawn, our main weakness and in certain lines, after Qe5 we create tactical possibility of f5. Here, any other move than Nd5 gives us nice game after Qe5 as then any Nd5 ideas are just not that good as our king can run away to f8. } { [%csl Be5,Gg8,Gc8,Bf6][%cal Bc5e5,Ge8g8,Ge8c8,Bg7f6] } 17. Nd5!! { Best move } 17... Kf7! { We sidestep any issues and now exd5 is a real threat } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be6d5] } 18. Nf4 { And this is white's point. The knight on f4 is just a better placed piece now. However, I think its quite a doubled edged sword as now, white's king is even less protected. } 18... Qe5 19. Rf1 a5 $13 $40 { To me, position seems quite interesting to play. Our main idea is to shave the a-pawn down the white's throat. Our other idea is to play Bh6 to undermine the f4 knight and then if g3 h4! is a nice idea. Also, well timed f5 can be quite problematic for white. The main tactical issue in our position is the Nxe6! idea to then follow up with some king of Bc4.
Here, white must be rather careful as e.g. 20. c3?! runs into 20... b4! 21. cxb4 Rab8! with good chances for black. Also, I think here is were you can finish memorization. We are on move 19, so really deep and from now on, our moves come quite naturally. One idea you should know is Rab8, to prepare tactics on b2 pawn and have an easy way of playing against Qf2-a7+ or -b6 ideas.
Nonetheless, I will show you the best way white can play to make a point - its hard to play as white and there is no way to get an easy, stable advantage here. } { [%csl Ba3,Gf5,Gb2,Bf4,Re6,Rf7,Bb8][%cal Ba5a4,Ba4a3,Gf6f5,Gg7b2,Bg7h6,Bh6f4,Be5f4,Rf4e6,Rd3c4,Rc4f7,Ba8b8] } 20. Qe2! { Only move for a decent game - prevents b5 on the account of Nxe6!! and targets our h-pawn. Now, in the next few moves white must play only moves, which are extremely engine like or otherwise, he will just get mated. That's why I like this like practically. This is best white can get - a minefield were the light at the end of the tunnel is an imbalanced endgame. } { [%csl Bb4,Re6,Rf7,Bh5][%cal Bb5b4,Rf4e6,Re2c4,Rc4f7] } 20... Rab8! { Idea is to prepare various threats on b2. Also, white's main source of counterplay is Qf2-a7+ or -b6. We can stop both of these threats with Rb7. Now, I have a task for you. What is the plan for white in this position? There are no weaknesses, 21. c3? runs into 21... b4! and there is no counterplay here. What you will see here is just engine analysis to make sure that if by some miracle someone goes into this, we know that this is just fine for us } { [%csl Ga7,Gb7,Bb2][%cal Ge2f2,Gf2a7,Gb8b7,Ba5a4,Ba4a3,Be5b2,Bb8b2,Ba3b2] } 21. Rde1 $140 { This is engine's idea. The plan is to sacrifice the e4 for activity. } { [%csl Ge5,Be4][%cal Ge1e5] } 21... a4 { We continue with our plan. Now, 22. a3 runs into 22... b4 with massive attack. } { [%csl Ba3][%cal Ba4a3] } 22. Qf2 $7 { This move defends against a3 on the account of Qa7+ } { [%csl Ba7,Ba3][%cal Bf2a7,Ba4a3] } 22... Rb7 { We prevent Qa7. Now, the threat is a3. Here, white has one and only move, which to me is borderline un findable } { [%csl Gb8,Ga7,Ba3,Gf5,Gb2][%cal Gh8b8,Bf2a7,Gb7a7,Ba4a3,Gf6f5,Gg7b2] } 23. Be2 $7 { White intends to play Nd3, defending both the mate and b2 square. Now, the question is - didn't white just hang a pawn? Well, he will get counterplay for that (and h5 is weak so its eye for eye) } { [%csl Bd3,Ge5,Gb2,Rh5][%cal Bf4d3,Gd3e5,Gd3b2,Re2h5,Rf4h5,Bh8h5] } 23... Bxe4 24. Bxh5+ { The most logical continuation. } (24. Bf3 { Uses the fact that our bishop is pinned } { [%csl Bd3,Ge4][%cal Bf4d3,Gf3e4] } 24... d5! $13 { Here, we end the line with a note that its extremely unclear.. On the one hand, b2 is a huge weakness. On the other hand, white has everything pointed at our king. We are extremely deep in the woods here, but its just to show you that you can get a game and that the positions are rich and extremely hard to handle for white. This is best they can get. In this position, we should always consider sacrificing and exchange as then, we will get a pawn and a minor piece for a rook. If we account for the fact that b2 is weak and for our centre, its worth it. } { [%csl Ba3,Bb2,Gf5][%cal Ba4a3,Be5b2,Gf6f5,Gg7e5] }) 24... Rxh5!! { A nice tactical shot. If white goes for 25. Nxh5?? then 25... a3 26. bxa3 b4! gives us decisive attack. } { [%csl Bb2,Gh5][%cal Ba4a3,Be5b2,Ba3b2,Gf4h5] } 25. Rxe4 $7 { Only move, after we take on e5, we don't have mate threats } 25... Qxe4 26. Nxh5 { Here, both 26... f5 and 26... Qe5 are playable. } { [%csl Gf5,Be5][%cal Gf6f5,Be4e5] } 26... Qe5! $10 { And here, we end the analysis. We are left with this quite imbalanced endgame. On the one side, we have nice pawn centre and a central passed pawn. On the other hand, white has good passed h-pawn. Moreover, our king is more exposed, but in all king and pawn endgames, it's just way more active. Engine evaluates this position as equal but there is still a lot to play for. On depth 33 its +0.1, so its fair to say, its equal. } { [%csl Gf6,Bd6,Be6,Bc2,Ga3][%cal Ge5f6,Bb7c7,Bc7c2,Ga4a3] } *


[Event "Classical Sicilian: Open Sicilian: Sozin Attack"]
[Date "2026.02.16"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B89"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Sozin Attack, Main Line"]
[StudyName "Classical Sicilian"]
[ChapterName "Open Sicilian: Sozin Attack"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/TJY2SPbI/MNSXp4zc"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/zumek"]
[UTCDate "2026.02.16"]
[UTCTime "18:57:23"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bc4 { The second best line against Classical, so called Sozin Attack. Here, we enter the Velimirović Attack. Now, here we have at least 5 decent options, so if you are not fond of my recommended line, feel free to take a look at e.g. 6... Qb6 or 6... Bd7 with 7... g6 (or 7... e6 if white plays in a Yugoslav style and you are scared of it) and even 6... g6 as transposing into Yugoslav Attack with Bc4 is not really possible for white here. I mean, even 6...e5 and 6... Na5 are decent tries and black has an extremely good score in them. } { [%csl Bf7][%cal Bc4f7] } 6... e6 { The most sensible move. We make sure that white cannot occupy the d5 square. Be careful though as Nd5 is a potent sacrifice, especially if white has a rook on e1 and we haven't castled as then after our exd5 exd5 comes with a check. Now, an important thing in comparison to najdorf is that we haven't played a6. My general recommendation is quite uniform in a sense, we kinda do the same thing. However, there are three main ways of playing these lines
1) Expand on the queen's side with a6-b5. Make sure your c6 knight is protected, so Bd7 is useful. Then, Na5 is a good idea to either play Nc5 or grab the bishop pair (if they played Bb3). This is even better if they are forced to play axb3.
2) Play Be7 and Qc7, then your c6 knight is defended so you can play Na5 and b5. This is more classical way of playing this, but more on that later.
3) Just develop with Be7 and O-O. Then, either go black to the plan 1) or go for the break in the centre, either e5 or Bd7, Nxd4 and Bc6 with idea to play d5. Which is better depends on the position, but if white has played f4, then the break with e5 can be quite good. Also, sometimes Nxd4 and then b6 with idea Bb7 is good. Ne5 is a fine idea sometimes too.
Now, I won't go that much into theory here, as I think its more important for us to know the ideas and plans, not exact move order. One thing you must know is that white's main tactical idea in these positions is the Bxe6!! or Nxe6!! sacrifice idea. It is quite common in Najdorf, that white played Bxe6 and then Nxe6-xg7+ (if the bishop moved from f8 and black hasn't castled yet). Just know that this is an idea and we want this light squared bishop to defend e6 for the time being. One nice thing about Classical is that this sacrifice rarely works in comparison to some mainlines in the Najdorf. } { [%csl Rd5,Re8,Be6][%cal Bc4d5,Rc3d5,Rh1e1,Re1e8,Bc8e6,Bc4e6] } 7. Be3! { The mainline, white intends to go for the Velimirović Attack with Qe2 and O-O-O } { [%csl Ge2,Gc1][%cal Gd1e2,Ge1c1] } (7. Bb3 { Here, we will take a look what if they just castle and don't go crazy. This is how Fischer played this line back in the day. Now, today it is not seen as the most critical try, however in my database, 3 top games were played by Fischer and he won two of them, so I think its rather important to see how he played these lines } 7... a6!? { Here, we still don't know what white wants to do. It could be the case that they want to go back into the Be3 and Qe2 lines. Therefore, we stay flexible. The move a6 is almost always useful in these lines as it prevents Nb5 ideas and prepares b5. Now, if 8. Be3 Bd7! transposes into 7. Be3 line). In all before mentioned games, Fischer's opponents played 7... Be7 and then Fischer played 8. Be3. With this move, we deal with that issue. } 8. O-O { Seems to be the most natural move by far. Now, the main way white plays these structures is to put pressure on e6 pawn. The whole idea for white is to put pressure on e6... so what if we take away that plan from them? } { [%csl Bf5,Ge6][%cal Bf2f4,Bf4f5,Gb3e6,Gd4e6,Bf5e6] } (8. f4!? { The most aggressive approach, White claims a6 is too slow and wants to punish us for that. Here, we will stick to our main approach in these structures. White wants to play f5 and pressure e6 pawn, so once again, we render white's set-up unable to do so. } 8... Na5! { Here, 9. O-O tranposes to the 8. O-O Na5 9. f4. Our idea here is to take that bishop at an appropriate moment. Note that 9. e5 is fine, but we can respond in a similar manner like in the before mentioned line so, but we don't have to (also, white has some weird tricks in that position as in the 9... dxe5 10. dxe5 Bc5 11. Be3 Nxb3 12. axb3 Nd5 white has 13. Nxe6!! as Bxe3 doesn't come with check. Black is fine here as we have Qh4+ in many lines, if you were curious) and simple 9... Nxb3 10 axb3 Nd5! works fine too. Just note that 11. Nxd5?! runs into 11... dxe5!. There are many ways to play here and its really rare, but just to show you that these lines gives us more options than the mainline and that what we already know give us here more than enough for a good game. I mean, even the simplest 9... dxe5 10. fxe5 Nd7! is good for black. } { [%csl Gb3][%cal Ga5b3] } 9. f5 { The most popular and "in style" move. Here, once again, we will argue that we can play like in the main line, so argue we can get a good Boleslavsky structure, but the fact that we didn't get b5 in gives us an option. We don't have to worry about Ndxb5 as in the main line, so why even give white this weakness of d5 square? } 9... Nxb3 10. axb3 Be7! { And we castle next, maybe play e5 and b5, going back into the Boleslavsky structure (just be aware of Nxb5 tricks), maybe we could go for Bd7 and b5 stuff (same note as before). The main thing about this position is that we are fine, e6 is not hanging (as our bishop is still on h3-c8 diagonal).
There is one funny trick and a tranposition to a well known line, so I will give you this here, but here, we will play on ideas mostly } { [%csl Gg8,Be5][%cal Ge8g8,Be6e5] } 11. Qf3 { A common move. White still doesn't show us where he wants to castle and prevents b5 ideas. } { [%csl Be1,Ra8][%cal Rf3a8] } 11... O-O! { By far most logical move. Now, we can start thinking about e5 and d5 ideas. Note that 12. g4 runs into 12... d5! 13. e5 Ne4!! and we give away a pawn, but white has to deal with Bh4+ ideas and good luck finding a safe place for this king } { [%csl Be1][%cal Bd8h4,Bh4e1] } 12. Be3 { And here, we can go for before said tranposition } 12... e5 13. Nde2 b5! $10 { Here, it seems like white can take on b5, buts its a trick and to see white, see a referance game. In a nutshell, if 14. Nxb5? then 14... d5! is quite strong as white's king is still in the centre.
Now, if white cannot take on b5, then we will get 14... Bb7 and then, there is no Nxb5 ideas and we get more or less quite similar position to the one analyzed in the 8. O-O line. We will try to get d5 in and e5 is just quite weak and black must always look our for moves like b4, undermining the defence of that pawn. } { Akopian, Vladimir - Anastasian, Ashot, 0-1, URS-ch U26, 1989, https://lichess.org/vSqGt9to } { [%csl Bb7,Gb5,Gd5,Bb4][%cal Bc8b7,Gc3b5,Gd6d5,Bb5b4] }) 8... Na5! { Simple, yet effective. We plan on taking on b3, playing Be7, O-O and we timed b5 with Bb7. We get improved Scheveningen as white doesn't have his bishop on d3, which makes many attacking ideas quite hard to make work. Now, our next move is most likely b5 as we want to get Nxb3 and Bb7 (more on that later) } { [%csl Bb3,Ge7,Gg8,Gb5,Bb7][%cal Ba5b3,Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Gb7b5,Bc8b7] } 9. f4 { Best and most logical. Now, we could take on b3, but for now, I don't think we need to rush as that bishop isn't goin anywhere and white must constantly worry about that. Now, notice that this e4 pawn is quite weak. There is one quite common idea for black which we can go for } { [%csl Be4] } (9. Bg5!? { This move is quite tricky as its stops our b5 idea as 9... b5!? runs into 10. Nd5!!. Black isn't losing or anything, but its quite annoying as best move in that position is to take a piece and hold so 10... exd5 11. Bxd5 Bb7 12. Qf3 and this is just a mess. So this whole idea works, cause our knight on f6 is pinned } 9... Be7! { Seems the most logical, to unpin our knight. Now, we are ready with O-O and Nd5 doesn't work } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 10. f4 { Again, a tricky move. Our move 10... b5?! still doesn't work cause 11. e5! is really strong our typical 11... dxe5 12. fxe5 Bc5?? runs into beautiful 13. Bd5!! and we cannot take with the knight (obviously, its pinned) and 13... exd5 14. exf6 and this position, with our king in the centre, is abysmal. This move Bg5 has a lot of venom and I would suggest waiting with many b5 moves as concretely, this pin is often quite annoying } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } 10... O-O! $15 { Most logical. Now we are ready with b5. Now, white can play in many ways here, but what we should know here is that our chances lie in the superior endgame (better pawn structure and often better king) and with b5 and Bb7 where we have a lot of pressure on e4 pawn. Often, h6 is useful move (quite common theme in classical sicilian) so that white either gives us the bishop pair with Bxf6 or plays Bh4 and then his bishop is undefended. } { [%csl Gb5,Bh6][%cal Gb7b5,Bh7h6] }) 9... b5 { Our idea is simple - take on b3 and then play Bb7. We must first take on b3 as white has Bxe6!! ideas in many lines. It's quite for white. As I said before, white's main idea is to put pressure on e6. Therefore, first take on b3 (less pressure on e6) and then play Bb7 (as then, our bishop doesn't have to defend e6 pawn.. To show you why sacrifices on e6 are strong, take a look at this line: 10. Be3 Bb7? 11. Nxe6! fxe6 12. Bxe6 Be7?? and this is a blunder as white can just blow up the centre 13. e5!! and with Nd5, Re1 etc incoming, I think this shows well why these lines are worrisome for us. } { [%csl Be4,Gb3,Re6][%cal Bc8b7,Bb7e4,Bf6e4,Ga5b3,Rb3e6,Rd4e6] } 10. e5! { Only move which makes white's play somewhat sensible } (10. f5?! { More popular, but here we get a good boleslavsky structure } 10... e5!? { At first, it may seem wierd, but white cannot really hold onto the d5 square. We will take on b3, in all lines e4 will be weak so Nd5 is always a bit tricky and white's king is susceptible to checks e.g. Qb6+ } { [%csl Bb3] } 11. Nde2 { Common idea in many sicilians, to play Nd5 and Nec3. } { [%csl Gd5,Gc3][%cal Gc3d5,Ge2c3] } 11... Nxb3! { Seems logical as our main weakness is d5 square } { [%csl Bd5] } 12. axb3 { Now, 13. Nxb4 is a threat so we must do something about it } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bc3b5] } 12... Bb7! $15 { Black here have at least a slightly better position. Its hard to defend e4 pawn, Qb6+ is in the air and I have no idea how white is stopping d5 so yea, positions seems really good for us. Its quite important to know that if black gets d5 in these structures, he is rarely worse as f4 push often overextends white's position in subsequent structure. } { [%csl Be4,Gb6,Ge7,Bd5][%cal Bf6e4,Bb7e4,Gd8b6,Gf8e7,Bd6d5] }) 10... dxe5 11. fxe5 { Here, we must know a move. Its quite important to play this as we are in some otherwise due to white's pressure on frankly everything } { [%csl Rf7][%cal Rd1h5,Rh5f7,Rf1f8] } 11... Bc5 $7 { Only move, we put pressure on d4 and now, if 12. exf6?? Qxd4+! 13. Qxd4 Bxd4 14 Kh1 Bxf6 gives us better position as we will get the bishop pair, centralized king in the endgame and a pawn. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4,Bd8d4] } 12. Be3 Nxb3 $7 { Again only move. Our idea is that we need to take d5 under control. The bishop on b3 was making Nd5 awkward and now, we can play this move without a hush. } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bf6d5] } 13. axb3 { Seems more natural as now, white has some threats on b5 pawn. } { [%csl Rb5,Ra8][%cal Rc3b5,Ba6b5,Ra1a8] } 13... Nd5! { Again, the only sensible move in the position. Now, if white takes on d5 (most popular) after 14. Nxd4 Qxd4 we are just going for a great sicilian endgame with a bishop pair. If they play something like 14. Bf2, now white has ideas of Ncxb5 so 14... Nxc3! 15. bxc3 Bb7 gives us fine play, bishop pair and better structure as c3 could be weak in many lines. Also, our rook on a8 is defended. } { [%csl Be3][%cal Bd5e3] } 14. Qf3! { Best move, uses the fact that our knight is pinned } { [%csl Ba8,Rf7,Rb5][%cal Bf3a8,Rf1f7,Rc3b5] } 14... O-O! { Now, white must be accurate as we have ideas to take on e3 and then win a piece on d5. } { [%csl Bd4,Ge3,Rb7][%cal Bc5d4,Bd8d4,Gd5e3,Rc8b7] } 15. Nxd5 Qxd5 16. Qxd5 exd5! $10 { And here, we end a line, Engine gives this equality, but this looks promising for black. We have the bishop pair, white has arguably worse structure (we both have isolated pawn, but white's pawns are also doubled) and generally speaking, I don't see why we would complain about this position. Moreover, its easier to target e5 than it is to target d5, so yea, I like black's chances here.
Also, remember that this is best white can get against our idea, so yea, I really like this for us } { [%csl Be4,Ge5][%cal Bc8f5,Bf5e4,Gf8e8,Ge8e5] }) (7. a3 { Primitive, but sometimes played. White just says "hey, you can't get my bishop". I mean, I wouldn't reinvent the wheel here } { [%csl Ga2][%cal Gc4a2] } 7... a6 8. O-O { Now, I would play in similar way to 7. O-O and to see more in detail how to play these lines, check that variation. } 8... Qc7! { General useful move, we set up some pressure on c4 bishop. Also, queen is better placed here as it fights for e5 square, steps aside any d-file stuff and bring black closer to connecting the rooks. } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Gc7c4] } 9. Ba2 { Most natural. Uses a3 to make sure bishop is safe } 9... Be7 10. Kh1 O-O 11. f4 { Here, we have the same ideas as in 7.O-O line. However, for some reason black has great score in this line in master's database, but tragic in lichess 2000+ or slower database. So let me show you the most basic idea you can go for. As we know from the mainline, white intends to put pressure on e6. So how we can make this harder for him? } { [%csl Be6][%cal Ba2e6,Bd4e6] } 11... Nxd4! { Just take away the piece which puts pressure on e6. Its that simple. } 12. Qxd4 Bd7! $10 { And here, our idea is to play b5, grab some space on the queen's side and our main idea why we play Bd7 is that we want to continue with Bc6 and Qb7 with pressure on e4. Now, b5 and Bb7 is fine, but I am a bit worried about leaving e6 pawn unprotected, so for now, I would just make sure its defended. Next, Rad8 and we plan to play d5 at some moment. The main idea for white is f5 so make sure it doesn't work. That's why the bishop on d7 is useful - we weren't able to grab white's light squared bishop, so we make sure it cannot do anything useful. } { [%csl Bb5,Be4,Bb7,Gd8,Gd5][%cal Bb7b5,Bd7c6,Bc7b7,Bb7e4,Ga8d8,Gd6d5] }) (7. O-O { This is not that popular at master level, but there is one independent line which I think I should cover. I don't think its a particularly good line, so I will keep this brief } 7... a6 { 8. Bb3 Na5 tranposes into line we know and 8. Be3 has borderline on independent value. We can continue with 8... Qc7! setting up Nxd4 ideas with threats on the bishop. If 9. Bb3 Na5! and we are in lines we know (not the same thing, but ideas are the same). Now, there is one idea, which is perhaps as anti our set up as line can get. Its not common, but I think I should show you how to play these structures. I think in general these are improved Taimanov's. On a side note, I love Taimanov, played it along the Kan for two years and in many ways, Taimanov and Classical are alike. It is often the case that one can get Taimanov from Classical so I think I should present to you some ideas in these structures. } 8. Be3 { Develops a piece, white is flexible and our 8... Na5 allows 9. Bd3. Its fine for black, but I really want that bishop pair } { [%csl Gd4][%cal Ge3d4] } (8. Nxc6!? { Not common, but as of making this chapter, polish Grandmaster, Jakub Seeman got hit with this idea and was in some trouble (he is playing this game as of now). Therefore, I want to cover how to play this as I think, on lower levels, you may see this structure quite a lot } 8... bxc6 { I mean, to me this looks dubious. We will play e5 and be fine. Now, this position is not popular and white can play in many ways, so I will analyze just the most popular. just know that our main idea is to play d5 (important move, Seeman didn't play it and was worse), Be7 and Qc7. Sometimes, a5 and Ba6 could be decent } { [%csl Gd5,Bg3,Bh6,Ge7,Gc7][%cal Gd6d5,Bd1f3,Bf3g3,Bc1h6,Gf8e7,Gd8c7] } 9. Bg5 { Most common, just develop a piece on an active square } 9... Be7 { We unpin and prepare O-O. We still keep in mind option of d5 } 10. Qe2 { White intends to play e5. Its not that great, but it could be annoying, so we should see that this is an idea. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Be4e5] } 10... d5! { By far the most natural move. We have pressure on e4 and we have nice centre. Notice, that this move is a bit risky as we still have our king in the centre. White could try 11. Bxf6!? as now 11... Bxf6? runs into 12. exd5 cxd5 13. Bxd5! and the e-pawn is pinned. Therefore, we have to play 11... gxf6, then 12. Rad1 Bb7 preventing all ideas of "take on d5 with something". If you are not fan of this, you can start with 10... O-O instead, but black is fine here and likely, we will know how to handle this structure better than white. } { [%csl Be4,Re8][%cal Bf6e4,Bd5e4,Re2e8] } 11. Bd3 O-O! $10 { We continue with Bb7 (make sure there are no tricks with d5, also potentially c5 opening our bishop is a decent idea. Also } { [%csl Bb7,Ge5,Bh6][%cal Bc8b7,Gf6d7,Ge6e5,Gd7e5,Bh7h6] }) 8... Qc7! { A common Taimanov and in general Sicilian move. In this case we set up threats on the white's bishop. Notice that a6 prevents all Ndb5 ideas, something which can be sometimes annoying in Taiamnov (some modern lines rely on these moves, they are mostly harmless, but can be annoying). } { [%csl Gc4,Bb5][%cal Gc7c4,Ba6b5,Bd4b5] } 9. Qe2 { Maybe a bit weird, but sensible move. White defends his bishop and connects the rooks } { [%csl Gc4,Ba1,Bf1][%cal Ge2c4] } 9... Be7! { Now, for the most part, if they play something not covered in this course (which is somewhat likely as there are so many transpositions and random moves here e.g. white could play 10. Kh1 here and be fine. Its pointless to memorize all of these, you should understand the ideas. } 10. a4 { Loskutov, Oleg - Amonatov, Farrukh, 0-1, Moscow op-E, 2011, https://lichess.org/7JJJWC38 } { White stops our b5 and creates option of Ba2. So, Na5 isn't that good really as it does nothing (This move is still quite useful in many Taimanov's, especially if b5 is playable to get Nc4 by the way) and b5 is not playable. So what do we do? } 10... O-O! $10 { Here, for the sake of simplicity, you can castle. However, you don't have to play this move and to see why and what for, read bullet points below.
- Ne5 with ideas Nc4 or Neg4, sometimes even Ng6, playing against f4. Line to see this is: 11. Kh1 Ne5 12. Bb3 Neg4! attacking white's dark squared bishop 13. Bd2 d5! and we break in the centre. This is perhaps the most important idea in these structures. This idea is so nice that some people even sacrifice a pawn to get that move in (especially if white has played c4 too). A nice game to see why d5 opens up play for our pieces is game 16 in Karpov - Kasparov 1985 is a nice showcase of that: https://https://lichess.org/study/b6q7gDGK/WErGXxUY)
- Going for hedgehog set up with b6, Bb7 and Rc8. Solid and fine. Line to see that is: 11. Kh1 b6 12. f4 Bb7 13. Bd3 Nxd4! A nice idea. Mostly, in these structures we have our knight on d7 as Nxc6 can be at times annoying as it misplaces our pieces a bit. Anyways, main way to play this is to put pressure on e4. The classical way of doing so it to play rather weird Rc8, Qb8!?-a8. It looks weird but often makes sense, just make sure white doesn't have any e5 nor f5 first. Another way to play is just 14. Bxd4 Nd7! trading pair of minor pieces (we have less space, its a decent idea) and now, we prepares Bf6. Another common idea in these structures is to go for double finachetto so Rfe8 (this rook is also well placed as it prepares e5, another break along the d5) and then Bf8, g6 and Bg7. Then, its a lot harder for white to attack us with f5. A nice game to see this in action is last game of Karpov - Kasparov 1985 match: https://lichess.org/study/b6q7gDGK/hxxiv4PB
- Sometimes, we can omit playing short castle and go for h5! with idea to continue h4-h3 and create long term weaknesses on the king's side. Its especially good idea in hedgehog structures. A nice way to show this idea is this line: 10. a4 Ne5 11. Bb3 b6 12. Kh1 Bb7 13. f3 h5! with idea to take g4 square under control and prepare h4-h3.
- If white plays f4, then Nxd4 Bxd4 e5! is often a nice idea. Then Be6 and Nd7-e5 is a common way to play in many Kan sicilian or Najdorf Sicilian lines so e.g. 10. f4 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 e5! 12. Be3 Be6 and we play for the control over d5 square. Its a common structure in Rauzer Sicilian and we will talk more about it, but for now, I think you have enough to know how to play these lines. Your main idea is to play d5 at some point, sometimes you take on f4 and play Nd7-e5, sometimes Qb6+ is an annoying move. For referance game, see: Michiels, Bart - Amonatov, Farrukh, 0-1, Khanty-Mansiysk ol (Men) 39th, 2010, lichess.org/eWDr46LW
- Often playing for a central break is by far the best thing to do, so either e5 or d5 is good in many lines. Especially, d5 is really good if after e5 we can play Ne4.
- If white plays a5, stopping b6 Bb7, we can play Bd7 and then Nxd4 with subsequent Bc6. Then, its easier to get d5 in. Here I won't show an example line as white cannot get a5 in, but it is a general take on the position.
Now, I know its a lot to digest, but I want you to get the feel for the position and understand basic patterns so: aim for d5 or e5, perhaps play b6 and Bb7. If you find this too slow, play Bd6 and Nxd4. A nice way to play is Ne5 and put pressure on the c-file. Many of these ideas will be analyzed in subsequent lines (sometimes alternatives as I often recommend in many Classical Sicilians line to just tranpose to Dragon as its simpler, however I will add alternatives where we see more of than in action) } { [%csl Be5,Gb6,Gb7,Rh3,Gd5,Yd4,Bc8][%cal Bc6e5,Gb7b6,Gc8b7,Rh7h5,Rh5h4,Rh4h3,Gd6d5,Ge6e5,Yc6d4,Ba8c8] }) 7... a6 { We go for the plan 1) with quick expansion on the queen's side. One thing to note is that as black, we have many different move order to choose from, so if you need a secret weapon for an tournament, then you can find one here.
These positions were considered quite dangerous for black some time ago, but it seems to me that nowadays, black is doing just fine here (and master's database confirms that) } 8. Qe2 { Idea is that white wants to go for quick O-O-O and to threaten some e5 pushes (as then, their rook is looking at our queen) } (8. Qd2?! { Sometimes, you may see them going for Qd2. Its inferior as white now won't have the e5 push in many lines. } 8... Bd7 9. f3 b5 10. Bb3 { White intends to go for english style attack. Normally, we would play Na5, but notice that we went for Na5 after they castled. Moreover, with queen on d2, their attack is often just a bit quicker as their bishop-queen battery gives them quick g4-g5 in many lines. For, now, I would develop and wait for them to go for O-O-O } { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bg2g4,Bg4g5] } 10... Be7 11. g4 { What if they just won't castle? Well, we have an idea already known to us from Richter Rauzer } (11. O-O-O?! { Here, I wouldn't go for Na5 as white is quite well positioned for king's side attack with g4 as now, the bishop-queen battery gives them some nice attacking ideas (including sacrifices). I think we need to be more direct here. } 11... b4! { This is the concrete idea we want to go for. Note, that this move is only possible cause white put their queen on d2 as in the main line with 8. Qe2 (for explanation, check out that line) and here, white doesn't have the final move Qc4. } 12. Nce2 a5! $17 { And we just go for the attack. We have Ne5-c4 ideas, a4 ideas, maybe even d5 at some point, Rc8 and just blasting the queen's side } { [%csl Ba4,Gc4,Gd5][%cal Ba5a4,Gc6e5,Ge5c4,Gd6d5] }) 11... Nxd4! { We take on d4 so that white must put a piece on d4 and mis-coordinate his pieces } 12. Bxd4 O-O! $15 { And now, we will go for typical Sicilian attack with a5 and either a4 or b4. If you need a deeper dive into this type of position, feel free to take a look at Richter Rauzer Alternative chapter as these ideas are quite extensively analyzed there. Our go to now queen's side expansion and Bc6 (preps d5 in some positions) which allows Nd7-c5. } { [%csl Ba4,Gb4,Bc6,Gc5][%cal Ba6a5,Ba5a4,Gb5b4,Bd7c6,Gf6d7,Gd7c5] }) 8... Bd7!? { Before b5 we make sure our knight is defended. Some people have also started with Na5, its a fine line too. Feel free to play both of them. The benefit of Na5 is that we can still go for Bb7 if needed, however, now after Na5, Bd3 is a bit annoying.
Now, this is quite modern way of playing. The more traditional and way more natural plan of 8... Qc7 9. O-O-O Na5! 10. Bd3 b5!? with unclear game is extremely promising for black. Plans are simple to understand (play Nd7-c5, push b4 and if needed Rb8 and then continue with a5-a4, play on the c-file and wait with short castle to not give white a target, maybe push d5 or e5 at some point) is great option against unprepared, weaker opponents or in short time control. However, modern engines have found some insane lines (white plays only moves for 5 or so moves) to get like +0.8. If we want to make Classical Sicilian our main choice, we must assume that the player we will be playing against prepares for that. Therefore, if we choose this line, they will most likely study their ass off in these lines. Now, I think these lines are great in terms of practical value and I want to create (at some point, maybe when you are reading this, they already exist) an alternative with this classical line as I think with best play, we shouldn't be much worse (if not even equal) but I for now, let's see what top guys play nowadays here. } { [%csl Bc6,Bb5][%cal Bd7c6,Bb7b5] } 9. O-O-O b5 { Note that Bd3?! runs into quick b4! and Qa5 with massive attack on the queen's side. } 10. Bb3 { A nice trap white has here is that after 10... b4? white has 11. Nxc6! Bxc6 12. Qc4! and we are note equipped to fight now. } (10. Bd3? { This is quite common move in many Sozin positions as whtie anticipates Na5 and wants to defend his weak e4 pawn. The problem is, that in this position this common move doesn't work that well. In the classical Sozin, black plays Qc7, not Bd7 and therefore white has Na4 in many lines. Here however, white doesn't have that privilege. } { [%csl Be4,Gb3,Bd7][%cal Gc6a5,Ga5b3,Gc4b3] } 10... b4! { Now 11. Na4?? runs into 11.... Nxd4 Bxd4 12. Nxa4, winning a piece. } 11. Nb1 Qa5! $17 { Now a2 pawn is hanging, and we can get a nice initiative with Ne5, Rc8 and Qc7 with a5-a4-a3 ideas. Also, simple Be7 and O-O and then Rfc8 is a good idea too. We just get a dream sicilian here. One thing to note is to not be greedy here. We don't have to win the e4 pawn quickly, as then white gets counter play on the e-file as we haven't castled yet. Its not that its a bad move. Quite often, taking winning that pawn is the best move. My point is that if it feels like you have better things to do, then wait, at least play Be7 so you have quick option to play short castle. Don't think that this move is bad, just that you should be a aware of the fact that our king is still in the centre.
All in all, white's position seems quite depressing. } { [%csl Ga2,Be5,Ge7,Gg8,Bc2,Ga4,Re8][%cal Ga5a2,Bc6e5,Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Ba5c7,Bh8c8,Bc8c2,Ga6a5,Ga5a4] }) 10... Na5! { And here, we end the analysis. If you are curious if this position is practically pleasant for us, then just know that in lichess 2000+ blitz or slower database, black scores 62% and in master's database black scores 59%, so needless to say, black is doing fine here. Idea is to take on b3 (at some point, as white can in many positions just play cxb3!? as in the game Fischer - Larsen). One idea, which we play in like 95% of games in b4! to make sure there are no Nd5 tricks, to prepare d5 and just kick a well placed knight. Notice that our bishop on d7 eyes the a4 square, so Na4 is far riskier than in typical Scheveningen structures.
Our plan revolves around blowing up the queen's side so Rc8, Qc7 and b4 are our go to plans. White may try to go for the attack of their own with f3 and g4. Just remember that sometimes moves likes g6 in order to keep the king's side locked, combined with Bg7 (or Bf8-g7) can be a good idea. Generally, a nice idea against g4-g5 is Nh5 and g6. Same goes for breaks in the centre (sometimes, if we can get Bc6 and d5 is, then it could be a good idea.
Our next moves are Be7, O-O and b4. Note that quick 11. g4!? runs into 11... b4! 12. Na4 Nxb3 13. axb3 (13. cxb3 would be interesting, but still 13... Nxe4 works well) 13... Nxe4 with a pawn up position. White has some compensation as our king is still in the centre, but its hard to see how white would use that. This idea is also interesting if white plays 11. Kb1, but then white may have a touch more compensation as he didn't weaken his queen's side. Still, after 11... Nxb3 12. cxb3 b4! 13. Na4 Nxe4 14. Rhe1 Qa5!? with objectively equal position, but white must prove that they have enough for the pawn. The idea behind Qa5 is that now, there is no 15. Nb6?? Qxb6 16. Nxe6 Qb7! with idea to then play O-O-O as black. We also attack the a4 knight.
The most natural idea of 11. f3 is well met by the most natural 11... Be7 12. g4 b4! as its typical in such structures. I think this move makes a lot of sense and white must be quite accurate. I mean, they either go back with their knight and then after 13. Nb1? Nxb3! and white's king is stuck for now, so 14. axb3 Qc7 with decent play. Going back to our line, that's why 13. Na4 is better. Now 13... O-O. If something like 14. g5 Nh5 15. f4 g6! is fine for us. We can play Ng7 if needed and I don't see how white's attack is any scary for us. If more accurate 14. h4 Rb8 preventing all Nb6 tricks as we set up a nice idea 15. h5 Qe8!? and the knight on a5 is in quite precarious position. Now, this idea of Rb8 and Qe8 is quite nice and common. The move Qe8 itself I saw in Ben Finegold's game (I think he said once he read a book and its was a misprint or something). The move makes some sense and now, white must be really accurate, probably he needs some counter play with g5.
One thing is that if white plays 11. a3 we shouldn't take as we do NOT want to allow 11... Nxb3 12. cxb3. This is quite common pattern for white. Then, white can play Kb1, Rc1 and there is no attack for us in sight. 11. a3 is not ideal for white as it creates a hook.
Now. } { Huang, Renjie - Xu, Xiangyu, 0-1, ch-CHN Rapid 2024, https://lichess.org/DJz0iXYt } { [%csl Bb4,Bc2,Ge7,Gg8,Rb3,Ge4,Be8][%cal Ga5b3,Bb5b4,Ba8c8,Bc8c2,Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Rc2b3,Gf6e4,Bd8e8] } *


[Event "Classical Sicilian: Open Sicilian: English Attack"]
