[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/zumek"]
[UTCDate "2026.02.16"]
[UTCTime "18:13:16"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Be3?! { This critical line against the Najdorf is just mediocre at best here. Reason being, Nc6 is just far more useful move than a6 in many lines.
One thing I should mention, if you need to make this repertoire as theory free as possible, then just play e6 as you will most likely transpose into 6. f3 line. That being said, I think that we have a nice response here which will give you many easy wins. } (6. f3 { White intends to go for the English Attack, but improved as now we don't have our little Ng4 idea. } 6... e5! { I think this is the only move which gives us equality. I mean, sure maybe you can make something else work e.g. 6... e6 or 6... Nxd4, but I think thins move just gives us a nice and easy game plan. What we must be worry about is the d5 weakness and our main goal here will be to just get rid of it. } { [%csl Bd5] } 7. Nb3 (7. Ndb5?! { For some reason, most people play this against me. Here, we get just improved Sveshnikov. } 7... a6 8. Na3 { Here, the most natural move is b5, but thee } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bb7b5] } 8... b5 9. Nd5 Nxd5 { if 10. Qxd5 Qc7 with 11... Be6 is mind. The knight on a3 is misplaced and it takes at least 3 moves to get it back in the game (with c3, Nc2-e3) } { [%csl Bd5,Bc7,Ge6][%cal Bd1d5,Bd8c7,Gc8e6] } 10. exd5 Nd4!) 7... Be6 { Intending quick d5. Now, if they allow it, you can play d5 and just claim about equal endgame. In this course I will give you an idea which I believe gives you nice winning chances as I want this course to be useful for club level players with intentions to maybe go for the title. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd6d5] } 8. Nd5 { This is quite common for this line. I don't think much of it as we just get rid of the main weakness of the d5 square. Now, for some concrete reason we won't take on, we will wait for one move. Reason being we want to play 8... Bxd5 9. exd5 Nb4 but now, white has 10. Bb5+ and we cannot really move our king.
That being said, 8... Bxd5 is playable, but then just after 9. exd5 we play 9... Ne7. } (8. Be3 { Here, I will give you two options. One which equalizes quite easily and the other which keeps more pieces on the board (or just gives us the first line but up a tempo) } 8... Be7!? { My main recommendation. Shankland said in his course that he believes this move to be an inaccuracy, but I think thanks to some modern ideas, this is perfectly viable line. Now, if 9. Nd5 Bxd5! tranposes us back into 8. Nd5 lines } (8... d5! { There is more ambitious line here, but this gives us easy game and its recommended by GM Sam Shankland too, so this suggests that this move is a valid choice. If you need nothing more than a draw, just play d5. } 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxd5 Qxd5! { We just trade everything. If they don't trade with us, we just develop and go for either O-O-O or O-O, depending on the situation } { [%csl Gc8,Be7,Bf5,Bg6][%cal Ge8c8,Bf8e7,Bg7g6,Bf7f5] } 11. Qxd5 (11. Bd3 { Second most popular move in lichess 2000+ blitz or slower database } 11... Nb4! $15 { We just grab the bishop pair or trade the queens } { [%csl Bd3][%cal Bb4d3] }) 11... Bxd5 12. O-O-O O-O-O! $10 { I would end the line here. If they play 13. Bb5 then 13... a6 just to ask them what are they even doing. Besides that, we will just develop with Be7, maybe f6 or even f5 in some scenarios with idea to create a passed pawn in the centre. } { [%csl Bb5,Ba6,Be7,Bf6][%cal Bf1b5,Ba7a6,Bf8e7,Bf7f6] }) 9. Qe2 { [%csl Bc1][%cal Be1c1] } (9. Qd2?! { If they don't play accurate Qe2, then I see no reason not to play the most obvious move in the position } 9... d5! { We just get improved version of the 8... d5 alternative } 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 Qxd5! $10 { This position is at least equal and practically speaking, black scores exceptionally well, both in lichess and master database. }) 9... a5! { A new idea, we start a quick attack on white } 10. O-O-O (10. a4 { Some people may try this to prevent a5. } 10... O-O 11. O-O-O { Now black has a threat of Nc5, so we should move our queen } 11... Qc7 { If something like 12. Nb5 then 12... Qb8! with idea to play Rc8 combined with Nb4 } { [%csl Bb5,Bb8,Gc8,Gc2][%cal Bc3b5,Bc7b8,Gf8c8,Gc6b4,Gb4c2,Gc8c2] } 12. g4 Nb4! $10 { With idea to play d5!. This position has been barely reached in the lichess database so I wouldn't go any further. Just know that d5 is our main idea and we we can open up the the queen's side, we will. Often Rfc8-xc3 is a good idea to just soften up white's structure over there. We also sometimes play d5 as after exd5 we play (if we can) Bf5! just to put even more pressure on c2 pawn. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd6d5,Bd5d4] }) 10... a4 11. Nc5 { [%csl Ba4][%cal Bc5a4,Bc3a4] } 11... a3 12. b3 O-O! $13 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd6d5] }) 8... Be7! { That's why we start with Be7, we want to have Kf8 if needed. Now, if they take on e7, we are just fine. Its our bad bishop, white spent 3 moves to win a useless bishop which moved only once. } { [%csl Bf8][%cal Be8f8] } 9. Be3 (9. c4 { Trying to go for some kind of maroczy bind, but here I think white has weaken their dark squares too much } 9... O-O 10. Be3 Nd7! $10 { And we play along the dark squares with Nc5. We have also an idea to play Bg5 (quite common in these structures) to trade our bad bishop for their good bishop. Also, a5-a4 can be quite good to misplace white's knight even further. } { [%csl Ga4,Bc5,Bg5][%cal Ga7a5,Ga5a4,Bd7c5,Be7g5] }) (9. Nxe7?! { Quite dubious for reasons explained before } 9... Qxe7 10. Bg5 O-O { Now, if white doesn't stop us from playing Rfd8 and d5, then we will just go for this. } { [%csl Bd8,Bd5][%cal Bf8d8,Bd6d5] } 11. c4 { This is the only way I can make sense of white's play. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc4d5,Ge4d5] } 11... a5! $17 { We want to expand with a5, then h6 to ask white's bishop the question. If they keep the pin with Bh4 then Qc7 and Nh5-f4 is a good idea as we want to play along the weaken dark squares. } { [%csl Ga4,Bc7,Bf4,Bh6][%cal Ga5a4,Be7c7,Bf6h5,Bh5f4,Bh7h6] }) 9... Bxd5! { Now we go for the before mentioned idea } 10. exd5 Nb4 { White must defend their pawn, otherwise we will just take it } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gb4d5,Gf6d5] } 11. c4 a5! $10 { And here we will go for the plan to play along the dark squares with Na6-c5, b6 (to make sure this knight is well defended). This, combined with Nd7 (or Ne8) and f5 is our main idea in this position. Also, a4 may be quite annoying for white in certain positions. } { [%csl Gc5,Gb6,Bf5,Bd7][%cal Gb4a6,Ga6c5,Gb7b6,Bf7f5,Bf6d7] }) 6... Ng4! { Direct idea. White now has to do something with their bishop, otherwise we will just take it and claim that we have the bishop pair } 7. Nxc6 { Most common } (7. Bg5 Qb6! { A nice idea, threatening some nasty stuff. Most notably, 8. Nxc6?? runs into 8... Qxf2#. White also cannot also just reinforce this knight with either 8. Be3 as then 8... Nxe3 -= nor 8. Nce2 as 8... e5! as the knight cannot move due to Qxf2# threat. White has one and only one move. } { [%csl Bf2,Gb2,Be5][%cal Bb6f2,Bg4f2,Gb6b2,Be7e5] } 8. Bb5! { Only move, counterpinning our knight } { [%csl Be8][%cal Bb5e8] } 8... Bd7! { Once again, white must deal with the threat } 9. O-O! { Rare best move, here we just go for the endgame, where we have no issues whatsoever } (9. Bxc6?! { Most common move } 9... bxc6 10. O-O Qxb2! { We just grab a pawn, now white must defend their knight } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb2c3] } 11. Qd2 h6! { Our plan is to grab the space on the king's side and fianchetto our bishop } { [%csl Gg5,Ga1][%cal Gg7g5,Gf8g7,Gg7a1] } 12. Rab1 Qa3 13. Bh4 g5! { Here, I would end the line with note that we want to play Bg7, O-O and just chill, maybe Ne5 too to make sure this knight doesn't hang in any line (also, then Nc4 is an idea). If white tries to prevent us from castling with e.g. 14. Bg3 Bg7 15. Rb7 then 15... Rd8! is good with subsequent short castle. } { [%csl Gg3,Bg7,Bg8,Rb7,Rd8][%cal Gh4g3,Bf8g7,Be8g8,Rb1b7,Ra8d8] }) 9... Qxd4 10. Bxc6 Qxd1 11. Bxd7+ Kxd7 12. Raxd1 Rc8! $10 { We just play this endgame with few notes
1) We want to get Ne5, so if they play move like e.g. 13. Bf4 then 13... f6! with idea that 14. h3 Ne5!
2) h6-g5 with Bg7 is a good idea as we must develop
3) e6 (and then g5) and Be7 could be quite good sometimes. e6 prevents any Nd5 ideas.
Our plan is to develop and handle white's initiative and development, if we can do it, then we will have nice endgame due to the pressure down the c-file. } { [%csl Bf6,Be5,Gg7,Gh6,Gg5][%cal Bf7f6,Bg4e5,Gf8g7,Gh7h6,Gg7g5] }) (7. Bb5 { This is the mainline in the master's database. White's main idea is to get damaged pawn structure, but they want to argue semi-open f-file and development is worth it. Now, the line is quite forcing, so I won't analyze it deeply for now. } 7... Nxe3 8. fxe3 (8. Bxc6+? { Seems natural, but if white releases the pressure then we don't have to play Bd7 and can play more actively } 8... bxc6 9. fxe3 e5! $17 { A nice move. 10. Nxc6? Qb6! with threats on knight and b2 and e3 pawn. Next, Ba6 disallowing white from short castle. Our plan is just Be7, O-O and then, maybe d5 to open up position for our bishops, fight for centre and chill. Another plan is Rb8 and Qb6 with play on the b-file. } { [%csl Bf1,Ge7,Gg8][%cal Ba6f1,Bc8a6,Gf8e7,Ge8g8] }) 8... Bd7! { Quite forced, we need to protect our knight and any other move just runs into concrete issues e.g. 8... Qb6? 9. Nd5 with edge to white } 9. O-O e6 { Now, we must develop quickly. We anticipate Qf3 and therefore we play e6, not 9... g6 as 10. Qf3 and we don't have a good response. Thanks to e6, we have a nice resource } { [%csl Bf7,Bf3,Ge7,Gg8][%cal Bc6e5,Be5f7,Gd1f3,Be5f3,Gf8e7,Ge8g8] } 10. Bxc6 bxc6 11. Qf3 (11. e5! { [%csl Bf7,Ge5][%cal Bd1h5,Bh5f7,Bf1f7,Gh5e5,Gd6e5] } 11... Be7 12. exd6 Bxd6 13. Qh5 O-O! $10 { [%csl Be7,Gc7,Gb6][%cal Bd6e7,Gd8c7,Gd8b6] }) 11... Qf6!? $44 { The before mentioned resource. On first sight, white can win a pawn, but then we enter a horrible endgame for him. } 12. Qe2 (12. Qxf6 { Most natural, winning a pawn } 12... gxf6 13. Rxf6 c5! { A nice move as now white must go back because 14. Ndb5? runs into 14... Rb8 and we get a lot of pressure on white's queen's side after subsequent Bg7. } { [%csl Bb5,Bb2][%cal Ba8b8,Bb8b2,Bd4b5] } 14. Nde2 Bg7! $17 { We get bishop pair, better structure, better knight, two semi-open files and many weaknesses to attack. Black gets more than enough compensation here and its really hard to not lose this as white } { [%csl Be5,Ge7,Gb2,Gg2,Bc6][%cal Bg7e5,Ge8e7,Ga8b8,Gb8b2,Gg8g2,Gh8g8,Bd7c6] }) 12... Qg5 { We keep our queen here. We want white to commit, to block the f-file so e.g 13. Nf3 and now either 13... Qd8 or 13... Qh6 and then Be7 and O-O and white cannot play Qf3 no more. Also, note that white's queen is stuck, defending e3 pawn. } { [%csl Bg2,Ge3,Rf3,Bf7][%cal Bg5g2,Gg5e3,Re2f3,Bg5g6,Bg6f7] } 13. Rf3?! { Mainline, intending Rg3 stuff. Here, we have a nice move. Notice that white cannot create mate threats on f7 now. } { [%csl Bg7][%cal Bf3g3,Bg3g7] } 13... Qd8!? 14. Raf1 f6! $17 { And here, we end the line. Next, Be7 and O-O gives us nice play. We can then push for d5 or e5. White can try stuff like 15. Rg3 with threats of Qh5+ in some lines and preventing us from moving our bishop, but ten simple 15... g6 is good for us. Then, we play Be7 and we chill. We can even wait with short castle and keep an option of long castle as white has all his pieces on the king's side. We can also just keep our king in the centre for now, being flexible. We have better structure, bishop pair and nice overall a good position here. } { [%csl Bg8,Ge7,Bg6][%cal Bf3g3,Bg3g6,Bf8g7,Be8g8,Gf8e7,Re2h5,Rh5e8,Bg7g6] }) 7... bxc6 { Best move, although in one blitz game I took on e3 and still got a fine position (don't do it at home though, its dubious). No } 8. Bf4 (8. Bd2 { Now, it is important to note that white can move their bishop anywhere and its pointless to remember everything, so what I would recommend is to learn the set-up recommended here and just roll with it. } 8... Qb6 { We make same double attack as before } { [%csl Gf2,Gb2][%cal Gb6f2,Gb6b2] } 9. Qe2 e5! $10 { And this is the set-up, more deeply analyzed in the mainline with 8. Bf4 }) 8... Qb6! { We create a double threat of both Qxb2 and Qxf2#. Note, that white must both defend f2 and somehow deal with a threat of double attack after Qxb2. To do so, their queen must defend both the c3 knight and f2 pawn } { [%csl Gb2,Gf2][%cal Gb6b2,Gb6f2] } 9. Qd2 { A funny mistake would be } (9. Qf3? { You may see this and there is a nice idea to know } 9... Nxf2!! { We sac a knight. If they do nothing we either take a rook on h1 or just go back to g4. If Na4 then Qb4+ picks up their knight. But what if they just take our knight? } { [%csl Bh1,Gg4][%cal Bf2h1,Gf2g4] } 10. Qxf2 Qxb2! $19 { And white loses one of the pieces. That's why the queen must defend the c3 knight. } { [%csl Bc3,Ba1][%cal Bb2a1,Bb2c3] }) 9... e5! { It's important to go for e5 as if we were to play 9... g6?! then white has 10. h3 Nf6 11. e5!. Therefore, we play e5 ourselves, we want to stop white from play e5.
Now, there are some concrete lines and sometimes black can or cannot take on b2. My general recommendation is that in blitz game, just grab that pawn and the longer the time control, be more sceptical as sometimes taking may not be so good.
Our general set-up is Be7, Be6, O-O and potentially break with d5 at some point, but it's not like we have to play this move as fast as we can. We can also expand on the queen's side with a5-a4-a3. } { [%csl Be7,Be6,Ga3][%cal Bf8e7,Bc8e6,Ga7a5,Ga5a4,Ga4a3] } 10. Bg5!? { This is a line, where we cannot take on b2. Why? Well, after 10... Qxb2?! 11. Rb1 Qa3 12. Nd5!! if 12... cxd5?? 13. Bb5+ and our position just melts down. Our king is in the centre and we cannot defend against this attack. } 10... Be6! $10 { This is pretty much always a good move, we prepare d5. Also, a good move would be h6 with idea to play g5 and then potentially to take on b2. However, white can defend this pawn with some Na4 ideas. I wouldn't worry about this. Now, if they don't defend this pawn now, then sure, just grab it as thanks to Be6 white doesn't have nearly as many attacking ideas as before.
One line which showcases this 12. Bd3? and now 12... h5 13. Bh4 g5! and 14. Na4?? runs into 14... Qxf2+ 15. Qxf2 Nxf2 and both bishops are attacked. However, if after 13... g5 they play 14. Bg3 then 14... Qxb2! is now quite good as the bishop blocks the d-file and white doesn't have nearly as much initiative as in the line shown before.
If they defend this pawn however, then either h6-g5 to just gain space or f6!? with idea to play d5! or just Nf6, Be7 and O-O. All of these are fine and you should be the one most sensible to you. } { [%csl Ge7,Gf6,Bh6,Bg5,Gg8][%cal Gf8e7,Gg4f6,Bh7h6,Bg7g5,Ge8g8] } *


[Event "Classical Sicilian: Open Sicilian: Miscellaneous"]
[Date "2026.08.10"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B73"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Dragon Variation, Classical Variation"]
[StudyName "Classical Sicilian"]
[ChapterName "Open Sicilian: Miscellaneous"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/TJY2SPbI/INZYUd8W"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/zumek"]
[UTCDate "2026.08.10"]
[UTCTime "18:41:32"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 { In this chapter we will take a look at some other lines. I don't think they are critical and It would be kinda pointless to pretend that I think much of them (depsite me playing one of these lines) so I just figured out we will take a look at all of them in this chapter. The common thing is that if white hadn't played for either Richter Rauzer or didn't play a move which in any way shape or form prepares Yugoslav Attack, then we just go for Dragon Sicilian. As Anish Giri said (or I misremember), Dragon Sicilian is the best sicilian by far, if we disregard the Yugoslav Attack. } 5... Nc6 { Now, } 6. Be2 { This is actually quite frequently played and decent line. Engine doesn't hate it but in practice, black has tremendous score here with 4 moves having 50%+ score for black. Therefore, I don't feel like making an entire chapter about it, especially when we can handle this in the same fashion as other lines. I will (of course) recommend you the dragon, however you can play here 6... e5 and 6... e6 (also 6... Nxd4 is playable with subsequent 7... g6) and its mostly a matter of taste. If you have prior experience with e.g. Kalashnikov, feel free to play 6... e5 if your understanding of these structures is superior. I play 6... e6 due to my Taimanov / Kan experiance but I here and there I will thrown in this 6... g6 move too. As I said in the introduction, I want to give you an understanding and ideas, not exact lines per se. Play what you are comfortable in, what you want to improve in and what you think is best. } (6. g3 { This is the line I play. Now, you may wonder, does white has here anything objectively? Well, not really, +0.1 or +0.2 at best, but the classical mainline ends up in -0.1 for black. White's set-up is based on the idea of containment. White just wants to make sure that they can either
1) take d5 under full control if we play e5
2) restrict and control entire queen's side if we go for the dragon
However, objectively, black gets a decent game in many ways. Playable moves include but are not limited to: 6... g6, 6.... e5, 6.... e6 (my favourite due to my Taimanov experience, this basically tranposes to the lines I already know quite well) and even a but dubious 6... Bg4!? (modern engines quite hate that move, but black scores here well in practice) } 6... g6! { Keeping it simple. We stick to the dragon formation if Yugoslav Attack is not playable (or is just mediocre) } 7. Bg2 { This move has an idea that if we play 7... Bg7 then 8. Nxc6 bxc6 9. e5! is quite strong. Therefore, we must solve this problem. Once again, problem is "if white can take on c6 then play e5" so we must make sure he cannot take on c6 } { [%csl Ga8][%cal Gg2a8] } (7. Nde2!? { Standard idea, reinforcing the c3 knight (quite important as white often play a4 and b3 to prevent any Na4-c4 plans) and potentially prepares some g4 and Ng3 ideas (playable, maybe not as common) } { [%csl Bc3][%cal Be2c3] } 7... Bg7 { No need to play something different, we continue with typical dragon } 8. Bg2 O-O 9. O-O { Here, white most likely wants to play b3 (prevents Na5-c4) and c4, move the rook to c1 and then Nd5 and Nec3 with nice positional edge. Therefore, we want to make sure this is not playable. Mostly, the move c4. } { [%csl Bd5,Gc4,Gc3,Gb3][%cal Bc3d5,Gb2b3,Gc2c4,Ge2c3] } 9... Rb8 $140 { We play for b5-b4 as then c4 is not a move. Its quite important to make sure to go for the queen's side play as fast as we can as if we don't white will just take away our counterplay, will play Nd5 and be better forever } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb7b5,Bb5b4] } 10. a4 a6! { We push and insist on b5 break. Note that 11. a5? loses a pawn after 11... Nxa5 } 11. Nd5! { Not the most common, but by far the best move. White tries to fight fire with fire - against our ideas of queen's side expansion, they go for active piece play.
Personally, I think this is maybe the best "anti-Classical" line out of all, especially if white doesn't want to go for theoretical lines of Richter Rauzer. } { [%csl Be7][%cal Bc1g5,Bg5e7,Bd5e7] } 11... b5! { We try to open up some lines on the queen's side } 12. axb5 { Here, apparently Rxb5 is playable, but I think axb5 is more typical of these structures (indeed, there are no games in masters database with Rxb5 and almost 100 with axb5). However, if you need a surprise weapon, Rxb5 is worth looking into. } { [%csl Bb5][%cal Ga6b5,Bb8b5] } 12... axb5 13. Bg5 Nd7! { We prepare our knight to reroute. Morevoer, h6 to push white's bishop back and e6 to kick white's knight are both important ideas. If they play c3 then b4! is strong and often, Bb7-xd5 is good just to get rid of this knight. Our plan is to keep the g2 bishop shut and make sure our g7 bishop puts a lot of pressure on white's queen's side.
Furthermore, just kick white back as now, they have some annoying pressure on our position. Feel free to check out the cited game as I think it showcases some ideas you should know from both white's and black's perspective. } { Oparin, G. - Artemiev, V., 0-1, Aeroflot Open A 2016, https://lichess.org/AxorkNHH } { [%csl Be5,Gh6,Ge6,Gb4,Bb2,Rd5][%cal Bd7e5,Gh7h6,Ge7e6,Gb5b4,Bg7b2,Rc8b7,Rb7d5] }) 7... Nxd4! { Now, there is no Nxc6. Moreover, white's queen will be on d4 square and we will be able to attack her with various knight discoveries. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bf8g7,Bg7d4] } 8. Qxd4 Bg7 { We continue in standard dragon fasion } 9. O-O O-O 10. Qd3 Nd7! $10 { Here, we have many ideas
1) Ne5-c6 with Qa5 and typical queen's side stuff known in the dragon. To know more about these ideas, study 6. Be2 lines.
2) Nc5 and then Bd7 with idea to play b5! and expand on the queen's side. Also, instead of Bd7, b6 and Bd7 with Rc8 is quite good too.
3) counter Nd5 with e6. Then, sure we have weak d6 pawn but their knight has no good squares where it could be placed (on c3, knight does more or less nothing).
Black has good chances here (in lichees 2000+ blitz or slower database, black scores 59%) } { Ponomarev, Grigori - Suleymenov, Alisher, 0-1, 43rd Nezhmetdinov Mem, 2021, https://lichess.org/uybUhgTr } { [%csl Gc5,Be6,Bd5,Gd7,Rc6,Bb6,Bb7][%cal Gd7c5,Be7e6,Bc3d5,Gc8d7,Rd7e5,Re5c6,Bb7b6,Bc8b7] }) (6. h3!? { An idea which tries to imitate the Adams Attack against the Najdorf. However, here thanks to Nc6 we get a really nice position.
Here, we have few options. Now, whether you will like my recommendation depends on if you consider h3 useful move in Yugoslav Attack. I, for reference, don't. To me, white more or less lost a tempo. Sure, maybe there are some minor benefits of h3, but in the grand scheme of things white always wants to push g4 and h4.
However, if you think h3 is beneficial for white in Yugoslav Attack, then feel free to just play e5. } 6... g6 7. Be3 Bg7 { Just typical dragon for now. } 8. Qd2 { Most common continuation in lichess 2000+ blitz or slower variation } (8. Bc4 { More common at master level. Idea is to go for some wierd Sozin Attack. I mean, I cannot think of a line where white plays h3 in the Sozin, so I think we just got a free move.
Now, note that for now we don't have central fork trick as 8... Nxe4?? 9. Nxe5 d5 10. Bb5! and our knight is not sufficiently defended and we cannot take on d4 as now, our knight is pinned. But keep this move in mind } { [%csl Be4][%cal Bf6e4,Gd6d5,Gd5e4,Gd5c4] } 8... O-O { Now, if they play e.g. 9. O-O we have this trick of 9... Nxe4 before mentioned. } 9. Bb3 { Sidesteps the threat } 9... Na5! $10 { And here I would end the line. If they go for by far the most popular O-O, then b6 and Bb7 utilizing our bishop pair to the fullest. I mean, I am generally fan of b6 instead of a6 and b5 and then moves like a4 at times can be annoying. However, if white goes for Qd2 and O-O-O, just go for that a6 and b5 with counterplay.
Also, this idea of Bb3 Na5 may look similar if you've studies the Sozin lines. } { [%csl Gb3,Rd2,Ra6,Rb5,Bb6][%cal Ga5b3,Rd1d2,Ra7a6,Rb7b5,Bb7b6] }) 8... O-O 9. O-O-O Nxd4!? $132 { Idea is to make white put the bishop on d4 so that they cannot play Bh6 with h4-h5 mating idea. This just buys us time. Also, we want to play Be6 to aim at a2 pawn and the knight on d4 is for this reason problematic (although, Be6 with Nxe6 is playable as then white doesn't have any Nd5 ideas).
The alternative is d5 which lands us in improved mainline of the Dragon. } { [%csl Bd4,Gh6,Be6][%cal Be3d4,Ge3h6,Gd2h6,Bc8e6] } 10. Bxd4 Be6 11. Kb1 Qa5! $10 { We will continue with Rfc8, Rab8 and b5-b4 in typical dragon style. Now, it seems quite clear to me that white would love to have f3 instead of h3 here as white doesn't have a counterattack on the king's side. Now, let's take a look at some Yugoslav ideas.
1) The most imporatnt one - Rfc8-xc3!! is perhaps the most important idea of all. If you can take that knight and then play Nxe4, its extremely likely its the best move. The idea is to double their pawns (so i guess if they cannot play Qxc3 then this idea is also quite sound), weaken their queen's side and then, in many scenarios we have easy target in the endgame (namely, the c3 pawn which is also attacked by the g7 bishop), we have c4 outpost and in many scenarios, the knight on c3 is the most important defender of white's queen's side so if we can get rid of it, we often have really strong attack
2) b5-b4 to dislodge the c3 knight as, once again, then white's queen's side is really weak
3) Various Nxe4 tactics as then our bishop opens up. Its quite common for this move to work e.g. in this position, if white plays 12. f4?? then 12... Nxe4!! 13. Nxe4?? Qxa2+! 14. Kc1 Qa1# and in general, now black's queen's side disintegrates.
At that's pretty much it. Now, many endgames are really good for you as you have nice pawn minority on the queen's side and besides that a healthy king's side pawn structure. So general guide is try to mate them before they mate you, and if you can't, aim for the endgame. } { [%csl Gb8,Gb4,Ga2,Re4,Bc3][%cal Bf8c8,Bc8c3,Ga8b8,Gb7b5,Gb5b4,Ge6a2,Ga5a2,Rf6e4,Rg7c3] }) (6. f4 { Common and natural move. Here, we can safely go for Dragon Sicilian. This move f4 is one of the more ambitious old moves against the Dragon which have been quite harshly treaded by modern engines. Nonetheless, we must know what to do instead we will be in a much worse position. Now, needless to say f4 is not a standard move in the Yugoslav as after f4, e4 pawn is much weaken and Ng4 ideas against Be3 work nicely.
One thing, if you don't like the 6... g6 recommendation, feel free to play 6... e5 (it's as good as 6... g6) or even 6... e6 (but this one could be a bit dubious after 7. Be3) } 6... g6! { Now, we enter the Levenfish variation of the Dragon Sicilian. I always remember this variation thanks to the Queen's Gambit show where Benny and Elizabeth play this variation (I believe Beth played this as white and Benny said he doesn't like facing it) in the car. Nonetheless, I think black has decent chances here. } 7. Nxc6 (7. Nf3!? { Quite a nice idea. I think here we must know our lines. White just claims, that this knight may be useful in king's side attack and more importantly, e5 could be an idea in certain positions } { [%csl Be5,Gd3][%cal Be4e5,Bf3e5,Gf1d3] } 7... Bg7 { note that 8. e5 Nh5 9. exd6 O-O! with completed development. White has numerous weaknesses e.g. f4 pawn and his king may be in some trouble quite soon } 8. Bd3 { It's a typical set-up against many sicilians where white aims for some king's side attack } 8... Qb6! { Not the only move, according to the engine there are numerous set-ups for black, but this one is quite annoying. They have pushed their f-pawn, so now we can just prevent them from castling } { [%csl Bg1][%cal Bb6g1] } 9. Qe2 { The most common and maybe best idea, to slowly preper O-O } { [%csl Ge3,Bb1][%cal Gc1e3,Ba1b1] } 9... O-O 10. Rb1 { Here, black can play typical Bg4 and d5!, but we have a different idea to, the one which seems the most natural to me (albeit to some of you may look quite risky). If you don't like my idea, just play 10... Bg4 with idea of eventual d5! } { [%csl Bg4,Bd5][%cal Bc8g4,Bd6d5] } 10... e5! $10 { Idea is to take d4 under control. Moreover, we may have some d5 ideas to sacrifice a pawn for open centre (as white's king is in said centre) and Bh6 ideas to target the f4 pawn. Sure, we get weak d5 square, but I think this dynamically makes sense. } { [%csl Bf4,Gd5,Bd4][%cal Bg7h6,Bh6f4,Gd6d5,Bc6d4] }) 7... bxc6 8. e5! { Perhaps the critical try. } 8... Nd7 { Its quite important that our bishop is still on f8 as now the d6 pawn doesn't hang } { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bf8d6] } 9. exd6 { By far the most common } (9. Bc4!? { Tricky idea, aims at 9... d5? 10. Nxd5!! trickery. } 9... Nb6! { Seems the easiest, now our queen defends the d6 pawn } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gd8d6] } 10. Bb3 Bg7! $15 { I don't think white has here anything, he is overextended, we will continue with 11... O-O with a fine position. Here, we have typical Dragon plan of playing on the queen's side, but here the e5 pawn is quite weak so white must deal with that problem too. Once again, I would aim for the endgame and in the meantime just use many weaknesses white has created. } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] }) 9... exd6 10. Be3 Be7!? $15 { The position is quite double edged, but black scores fine here. We play Be7 in order to still defend our d6 pawn. Next we play O-O and d5 (and maybe even c5). If white ever moves the f-pawn, then Ne5 is an idea. Also, Bf6-g7 is worth remembering (but if we play d5, then Bd6 is also a fine move). My general recommendation is to just aim for the endgame as then it will be quite apparent that white pawns are quite targetable and f4 just weakened white's position } { Popov, Ivan1 - Ulko, Jaroslav, 0-1, Moscow op 03rd, 2007, https://lichess.org/CA2kQKCC } { [%csl Bg8,Gd5,Bg7,Ge5,Bd6][%cal Be8g8,Gd6d5,Be7f6,Bf6g7,Gd7e5,Be7d6] }) 6... g6! { The Dragon Sicilian, now the only question we must answer is whether Be2 is any useful in the Yugoslav Attack for white. In my opinion, no. This bishop rarely moves and thanks to this move we have one tempo more. } 7. Be3 { Let's see what we shall do if white goes for this typical plan } 7... Bg7 8. O-O (8. Qd2 { As in the typical Dragon, white should start with f3, if he doesn't we can be quite annoying } { [%csl Bf2] } 8... Ng4! $10 { This more or less equalizes. Our idea is that white must give away his bishop pair here } 9. Bxg4 Bxg4 10. f3 Be6!? { May look wierd but there is a positional and dynamic idea behind this move. The dynamic idea is that in many lines, Be6 is a potent attacking piece as it creates threats along the a-file with the queen on a5. The positional idea is that if they take on e6, then after fxe6 we stop any Nd5 ideas and have 3 central pawns. } { [%csl Ba2,Ge6,Gd5][%cal Bd8a5,Ba5a2,Be6a2,Gf7e6,Gd4e6,Ge6d5] } 11. Nxe6 { Let's see concretely what is black's idea } (11. O-O-O Qa5! { We continue in typical dragon style, creating threats of Qxa2 (its not a threat now, but it is something white must remember at the back of their mind. } { [%csl Ba2][%cal Be6a2,Ba5a2] } 12. Kb1 Rc8! $10 { Next, we continue with Ne5-c4, maybe a6 and b5-b4. Notice that we don't castle. Idea is that we want to meat h4 and / or g4 with h5!. We want to play on all sides of the board, something quite common in the Sicilian (especially if you have experiance with the mainline Classical or certain lines of the Kan Sicilian). Of course, playing O-O is fine, but I think it makes a lot of sense to postpone this move knowing white can often autopilot Bh6 and h4-h5 idea (not that its works now, but it can be scary at times).
Now, few common ideas on this structure (I will give you this bullet point list here and in the 6. h3 line)
1) The most imporatnt one - Rfc8-xc3!! is perhaps the most important idea of all. If you can take that knight and then play Nxe4, its extremely likely its the best move. The idea is to double their pawns (so i guess if they cannot play Qxc3 then this idea is also quite sound), weaken their queen's side and then, in many scenarios we have easy target in the endgame (namely, the c3 pawn which is also attacked by the g7 bishop), we have c4 outpost and in many scenarios, the knight on c3 is the most important defender of white's queen's side so if we can get rid of it, we often have really strong attack
2) b5-b4 to dislodge the c3 knight as, once again, then white's queen's side is really weak
3) Various Nxe4 tactics as then our bishop opens up. Its quite common for this move to work e.g. in this position, if white plays 12. f4?? then 12... Nxe4!! 13. Nxe4?? Qxa2+! 14. Kc1 Qa1# and in general, now black's queen's side disintegrates. } { [%csl Gc4,Bh4,Bh5,Bb4,Rc3][%cal Gc6e5,Ge5c4,Bh2h4,Bh7h5,Bb7b5,Bb5b4,Rc8c3] }) 11... fxe6 { This looks bad, but the main idea here is that white no longer has Nd5. Its really hard to propose a plan for white here, which makes any sense except trying to pawn storm on the king's side } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Be6d5,Gc3d5] } 12. Bh6 (12. O-O-O? Qa5! $10 { Here, white is doomed. Idea is to force down a worse endgame down their throat. We want to take on c3, destroy their pawns structure (as then they have doubled isolated pawns on the c-file) and create marvelous outpost for our knight (Ne5-c4 idea). } { [%csl Gc3,Ba2][%cal Gg7c3,Ga5c3,Ba5a2] }) 12... Bf6! $15 { We keep our dark squared bishop. If white insists with 13. Bg5 then 13... Be5 if 14. Bf4?! then 14... O-O! and now if 15. Bxe5 Nxe5! -= we get a better endgame due to the open f-file, more central pawns, the c-file and a great knight. Notice how the e6 pawn prevents any Nd5 and now, white doesn't have any good squares for the knight } { Rubil, M. - Alvir, A., 0-1, Vienna Schev May 2023, https://lichess.org/apYQPLw6 } { [%csl Ga5,Bc8,Be5][%cal Gd8a5,Be8c8,Ba8c8,Bc6e5] }) (8. f3 O-O 9. Qd2 d5! $10 { The mainline idea in the Dragon. Now, the problem for white that in the mainline they have O-O-O instead of Be2, a far more useful move as here, white doesn't have the rook on d1 and cannot sufficiently fight against the d5 break. We threaten open up the centre and then, white will have really hard time creating any king's side attack } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4,Gf6e4] } 10. Nxc6 (10. O-O-O Nxd4 11. Bxd4 dxe4! { We just blast open the centre for our bishops } 12. fxe4 Be6! $10 { We get typical Dragon position, but we get the open d-file too, which could be quite useful for us. To see typical ideas in the Dragon Sicilian, check out 6. Be2 [...] 8. Qd2 [...] 11. O-O-O line or just 6. h3 line with O-O-O. Briefly, play Qa5, put rooks on c- and d- files (or c-d and b- files), push your queen's side pawn minority and either mate them with your bishops, queen and massive pressure put by your rooks or just go for nice pleasant endgame. } { Davidov, Alexandar - Silva, António Augusto M., 0-1, Interzonal TT/4, 2006, https://lichess.org/mISwu6gr } { [%csl Ba5,Gd8,Gc8,Gb4][%cal Bd8a5,Gf8d8,Ga8c8,Gb7b5,Gb5b4] }) 10... bxc6 11. e5 { Most common continuation } 11... Ne8!? { Of course 11... Nd7 is perfectly fine, but in some positions e6 can be quite annoying and I want our queen to look at the d5 pawn as there are times, where Nxd5 is scary e.g if 11... Nd7 12. f4 f6!? 13. Nxd5!! cxd5?? 14. Qxd5 picks up the rook in the corner. Now, this is not forced but It seems to me that Ne8 just doesn't have these issues } { [%csl Be6,Gd5][%cal Bc8e6,Be7e6,Gd8d5] } 12. f4 f6! $15 { White then have no central pawns, we have some nice open lines (namely the b- and f- files) to work with. We can centralize our pieces nicely with Bf5 (and maybe Ne4) and then Nd7 with e5!. We can also put our queen on a5 where it exerts some nice pressure on white's queen's side } { [%csl Gf6,Bb2,Be4,Ga5][%cal Ge8f6,Ba8b8,Bb8b2,Bf6e4,Bc8f5,Bf5e4,Gd8a5] }) 8... O-O { Here, we have nice dragon position. White is not worse here, but practically speaking, black's play is really natural. In the masters database black scores 56% and scores well in lichess 2000+ blitz or slower database too. We have few ideas here
1) We can break in the centre with d5. If we can do this and white doesn't get anything apparent in return, we have equalized
2) Typical queen's side expansion with Bd7, a6 and b5. Its quite common to go for this type of plan, Then, we can bring the heavy pieces with Qa5 and Rfc8 (or Rac8 ar the f8 rook often goes to e8 with one sole purpose, defend the e7 pawn against Nd5 ideas as they are often quite problematic) and then Ne5-c4 with marvelous position. Then, the game may take different directions, but a minority attack is not the worst idea in the world.
White often has one idea which is Nd5 and if we can make sure it doesn't do anything in particular, white's position is quite problematic to say the least (but of course, white is not worse here)
Let's take a look at two possible continuation to get a feel for these positions } { [%csl Gd5,Ba5,Gd7,Ba6,Bb5][%cal Gd6d5,Bd8a5,Gc8d7,Ba7a6,Bb7b5] } 9. f4 { White must always be careful with such moves as this drastically weakens their king's position } (9. Nb3!? { Best move, most common at top level and rare at amateur level. Idea is that white wants to next play 10. f4 and restrict our c6 knight. I have a rule of thumb which I used in Taimanov, which says "if Nb3 then Ne5-c4 and if f4, Na5-c4". Of course, the "f4 part" is a bit more nuanced, but if you can and you already have a rook on the c-file, its a good idea. Of course, we must just be sure there are no e5 etc.
Here, I would listen to that rule as after f4 it will be much harder for us to put something on the c4 square. Of course, other moves are playable like 9... Be6 (this is in fact best common move with idea to play Rc8 and Na5 and just trade these knights). } { [%csl Ba5,Ge5][%cal Bb3a5,Bc6a5,Gf2f4,Gf4e5,Gc6e5] } 9... Ne5! { Note that 10. f4 runs into 10... Neg4 11. Bd4 e5! 12. fxe5 Ngxf4 = where we end up in a nice position, with nice knight and just easy plans (Be6, Rc8 etc) } 10. h3 { This move sets up the f4 idea } { [%csl Bg4][%cal Bh3g4,Bf6g4,Be5g4] } 10... Be6! { We take c4 under control } { [%csl Gc4][%cal Ge6c4,Ge5c4] } 11. f4 Nc4! $10 { Here, I would end the line, we will reinforce this knight with Rc8 and a6-b5 (or Bd7 and b5) with queen's side pressure. Below, you can see a game which showcases how to play if white doesn't play h3 before f4.
Juzek, Adrian - Motylev, Alexander, 0-1, MK Cafe Cup-B, 1999, lichess.org/1UJkYjFB } { [%csl Bb5,Gc8,Bc4][%cal Bb7b5,Ga8c8,Be6c4,Be6d7,Bd7b5] }) 9... Qb6! { Immediately targeting white's pieces in the centre and b2 pawn. Now white must handle the threat of Qxb2 (as then there are 2 pieces hanging) and various tactics Nxe4 e.g. 10. Rb1?! Nxe4!! 11 Nf5 Nxc3! -= with a really good position for black. } { [%csl Ge3,Bd4,Gb2,Gg4][%cal Gb6e3,Bg7d4,Bc6d4,Gb6b2,Gf6g4,Gg4e3] } 10. Na4 { Only move which more or less keeps position equal } 10... Qc7! $10 { Now white's knight look dumb on a4. We continue with a6, Bd7, Na5-c4 and b5. Just be careful with b5 idea as sometimes, white can play Nxc6 and then after Qxc6 e5! with a threat on our f6 knight and Bf3 skewer tactic. Besides that, black has nothing to worry about.
White may try to go for Nd5 with idea that after Nxd5 exd5 they have the e-file to target our e7 pawn but we often have enough counterplay on the queen's side to at least compensate for that.
Westerinen, Heikki MJ - Miles, Anthony J, 0-1, Metz op 03rd, 1985, lichess.org/IghZBLrI } { [%csl Ba6,Bd7,Gb6,Gb7,Bc4,Ra8,Rc6][%cal Ba7a6,Bc8d7,Gb7b6,Gc8b7,Bc6a5,Ba5c4,Re2f3,Rf3a8,Rd4c6,Rc7c6] } *


[Event "Classical Sicilian: Closed Sicilian: Moscov & Alapin"]
[Date "2026.09.22"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B22"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Alapin Variation, Smith-Morra Declined"]
[StudyName "Classical Sicilian"]
[ChapterName "Closed Sicilian: Moscov & Alapin"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/TJY2SPbI/Ee0ag7Rp"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/zumek"]
[UTCDate "2026.09.22"]
[UTCTime "00:13:32"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 c5 { In this chapter, we will take a look at both Alapin and Moscow. The reason is, Moscow is really just few lines and it would be pointless to create a whole chapter for something which needs about 4 or so lines. I mean really, its mostly idea based and at below 2200 level, you don't need much. Also, a sizable chunk of moscow is just alapin in a wig, so it makes sense to combine these two. } 2. c3 (2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+!? { A modern try. Najdorf is quite good so nowadays people try to get a game by playing this move. This is, I think just a worse version of rossolimo for white. } (3. c3 { Here, white aims to play delayed alapin as now, we cannot play our standard d5! as we have commited to d6. However, here we have one additional resource. White cannot play e5 after Nf6, so I think you may guess what move we will play next. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bd2d4] } 3... Nf6! { Here, we obtain completely different type of position in comparision to the Alapin. This resambles more of a Moscow Variation than Alapin, that's why we analyze Alapin and Moscow in one variation - white just often plays in similar way in both. Now, the main idea here is that our main plan is to go for Dragon Sicilian style set-up. Now, I won't analyze every single move order as really, all we need to understand are ideas for black here. } { [%csl Bg6,Bg7][%cal Bg7g6,Bf8g7] } 4. Be2 { Most popular move, white prepares O-O. } (4. h3 { I think this is perhaps the best move. It directly prevents Bg4 ideas, which are rather annoying for white in many lines. Of course, as in 4. Be2 line, here 4... Nxe4?? 5. Qa5+ loses a piece. } { [%csl Bd1][%cal Bc8g4,Bg4d1] } 4... g6! { And as in all lines, our main concern is to get our dragon sicilian set-up } 5. Bd3 Bg7 6. Bc2 { Here, we can play in two ways. Either we ignore d4 and play 6... O-O 7. d4 and here either 7... d5 or 7... b5 or even 7... Nc6 inviting Benoni structure with interesting play. I will recommend you the most solid line as I think for club level players its just a bit easier to play for black. Although, if you must win, then I think the idea of 6...O-O 7. d4 Nc6 is perhaps the most interesting. } { [%csl Bd4,Ge5,Gb5][%cal Bd2d4,Ge7e5,Gb7b5] } 6... e5!? { We just want to take when they play d4. Simple as that. You may ask "don' we get IQP" and yes, but in it of itself its not bad and here, we can just trade it off and we get a really nice fianchetto bishop. Now, if they don't push play d4 e.g. 7. O-O O-O 8. d3 then 8... Nc6 9. Be3 Qe7 with idea to play for d5! (as Qe7 defends both e5 and c5) } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be5d4,Bc5d4] } 7. O-O O-O 8. d4 cxd4! { The point. We just trade, liquidate the centre and make sure we can open up some diagonal for our bishops. } 9. cxd4 exd4 10. Nxd4 Nc6! $10 { Next we push with d5 and I like this position for black as I always feel its easier to play with fianchetto bishop in these lines. We will get quick d5 and be fine
Zufic, Miroslav - Bologan, Viktor, 0-1, CRO-chT1A 18th, 2009, https://lichess.org/8vQJTqc8 } { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bd6d5] }) (4. Bd3 { Seems most logical if you've never seen any delayed alapin line. White prepares Bc2 and O-O. } { [%csl Gc2][%cal Gd3c2] } 4... g6 5. Bc2 Bg7! { We pretty much don't care. White is not prepared to play d4. Its quite important for white to play h3 move before d4. If you play Ruy Lopez you probably know that, but move Bg4 is really annoying if the pawn is on d4 (not d3 nor d5 by the way) as then, its just weak and there is no good way to defend it and moving it often just leaves important squares.
However, its the most popular line. Often, a mere threat of that move is extremely problematic for white
If white plays move 6. h3 then we can go to the plan we saw in 4. h3 line so 6... e5! } { [%csl Bh3][%cal Bh2h3] } 6. d4 { Most popular. I would recommend you to understand and remember this idea as its quite important in many lines } 6... cxd4 { A general useful move as we want to make sure d4 is weak. Also, now we have ideas of Nc6-b4 trying to grab the bishop pair } { [%csl Bd4,Gb4][%cal Gb8c6,Gc6b4] } 7. cxd4 O-O! { And now, we take a look at what white does. If he tries to go for well known for us at this point 8. h3 then we go back to idea analyzed in 4. h3 so 8... Nc6 9. Nc3 e5! is something we already know. Of course 10. dxe5 dxe5 is fine for us with Nh5-f4 and many easy ideas that just improve our pieces and put a lot of pressure on white e.g. Nd4, Qc7 with Rfd8, Be6 etc. Now, if more ambitious 10. d5 we have a nice trick, common in many openings 10... Nd4! 11. Nxd4 exd4 12. Qxd4?? runs into a nice trick 12... Nxe4!! and 13. Qxe4?? Re8! wins queen. } 8. Nc3?! { Most popular move. Now, its important to see that white cannot play Nbd2 supporting the f3 knight. If you see this, its a sign for you that our idea presented before is good to go } { [%csl Bc3,Bd2][%cal Bb1d2] } 8... Bg4! { And now, Nc6 is quite an issue as combined with e5, it puts a lot of pressure on d4. } { [%csl Gd4,Bd1][%cal Gb8c6,Gc6d4,Bg4d1,Ge7e5,Ge5d4] } 9. h3 { Most popular, to deal with the pin } 9... Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Nc6! { We attack the weaken pawn. We can put even more pressure with Qb6 and Nd7 if needed and now we can see that white is just lagging in development. } { [%csl Gd4,Bd7][%cal Gc6d4,Bd8b6,Bb6d4,Bf6d7] } 11. Be3 { Here, in comparison to 11. Qd1, the move 11. Be3 is more suborn as 11... Qb6 gives white one interesting option of 12. O-O-O!. Therefore, I think we can play another, quite common idea. } 11... e5! $10 { With simple plan - put a knight on d4. If 12. dxe5 dxe5 and we take with the pawn as we want to control d4 square. Also, Nxe5 gives us weak isolated pawn on d6, going back 13. O-O Nd4! is quite good for us as we get a strong knight, its hard to get rid of it as if we move the f6 knight, it can be supported by our bishop on g7. Generally, black in lichess 2000+ blitz or slower database scores here about 59%, so we have good practical chances. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4] }) (4. e5?! { For some reason, its quite common but here, we just get a better position here. } 4... dxe5 5. Nxe5 Nbd7! { And what we want to do is just trade. I mean, white moved this knight twice in the opening, If they take one more time, then we end up with more development than white. Now, if they don' take, we have a simple idea to just continue development with e6 and Be7 e.g. 6. d4 e6 7. Bb5 cxd4 as we don't want to move our bishop second time after Be7-xc5 8. Qxd4 Be7 with a nice position for us. Next, we play a6 to kick white back and put pressure on e5 knight. After we trade, then Bd7-c6 is a standard idea but more on that later
One funny idea is that after 6. Nf3 we can go for before mentioned set-up, but a nice move is 6... e5!? with more active play, then we can either play with double fianchetto or Bd6 and b6 and Bb7, we have many possible set-ups, but of course, typical 6... e6 is just as good. } { [%csl Be5,Ge6,Ge7][%cal Bd7e5,Ge7e6,Gf8e7] } 6. Bb5 { Most popular by some margin } 6... a6! { Again, most natural. White want's to be dynamic, but cannot. Now we ask him a question - what does this bishop do?
Note that 7 Nxd7?! axb5! 8. Nxf6+ exf6 gives us bishop pair and more space. We have just a better position here as white cannot use our doubled pawns and now, we have open lines e.g. the a-file and a2 is weak. } 7. Bxd7+ Nxd7! $17 { We insist on trading knights as we don't want to give away our bishop pair. Then, natural is Bxd7-c6, e6 and Be7 with fine play. I mean, there isn't much to comment here, we have easy to play position here. We can expand on the queen's side with Be7-f6 and b5! but we have more ideas her. } { [%csl Ge5,Ge6,Bc6,Be7,Bg8][%cal Gd7e5,Ge7e6,Bc8d7,Bd7c6,Bf8e7,Be8g8] }) 4... g6! { Here, we go for the Dragon Sicilian position. White also plays against Bg4 ideas as if he wants to play d4, he most likely doesn't want to be hit with Bg4 (more on that later). There is also a trick, if 4... Nxe4?? 5. Qa4+ wins a piece for white. However, note that if white now plays 5. d4? Nxe4 works as there is no Qa5+ idea } { [%csl Bg7,Bg8,Gc6,Re4,Re8][%cal Bf8g7,Be8g8,Gb8c6,Rf6e4,Rd1a4,Ra4e8,Ra4e4] } 5. O-O Bg7 { We continue with our set-up. One important idea to note is that we often play e5 in these positions and we turn it into much better King's Indian or Benoni. } { [%csl Bg8,Bc6,Ge5][%cal Be8g8,Bb8c6,Ge7e5] } 6. Re1 O-O 7. Bf1 Nc6! $10 { And here, our plan is to play e5, Bg4 etc. Here, we will either get King's Indian structure after 8. d4 cxd4 9. cxd4 Bg4 10. d5 Nd4 or Benoni structure with 8. d4 Bg4 9. d5 Ne5. It's not something we see often, but these structures are really easy to play for us and I see no reason to not recommend this line.
If we get e5 and white doesn't play d4 then perhaps h6 and Be6 with idea to either play a6 with b5 or Nh5-f4 and f5.
If white plays d4, then Bg4 and e5 are really nice combo.
Becerra Rivero, Julio - Najer, Evgeniy, 0-1, World op 36th, 2008, https://lichess.org/B0vNnBTS } { [%csl Be5,Gg4][%cal Be7e5,Gc8g4] }) 3... Bd7! { I think this is the simplest response. Now, depending on what they do we most likely will get either a good french or good maroczy bind. Either way, I think we are happy.
Now, there are many possible move order and I think its pointless to examine all of them. Therefore, I will only take a look at ideas
1) What if white plays for c4 i.e. Maroczy Bind
2) What if white plays for c3 and d4 like in Alapain
3) White if white goes back to d4 for wierd Open Sicilian.
We will often use our knowledge from previous chapters especially when we go for Dragon set-up (here, I won't outline plans about certain positions in such detail as they are pretty much the same as in Open Sicilian) } 4. Bxd7+ (4. a4 Nf6 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf6e4] } 5. d3 Nc6 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Be4e5,Gc6e5,Gd6e5] } 6. O-O g6 7. Re1 Bg7 8. Nbd2 O-O! $10 { [%csl Bc7,Gd5,Ga6][%cal Bd8c7,Gd6d5,Ga7a6] }) 4... Qxd7 5. O-O Nc6 { Now, in most cases they will either play c3 or c4. Either we get good french or good maroczy bind. } 6. c3 { White goes for Alapin style position } (6. c4 { White with this entere the maroczy bind } 6... Nf6 7. Nc3 g6! { Seems the most natural to put the bishop on the long diagonal. } { [%csl Ga1][%cal Gf8g7,Gg7a1] } 8. d4 cxd4 9. Nxd4 Bg7! $10 { And here, we enter quite rich positional battle. White's main idea is to plant the knight on d5 and just suffocate us. Our main counterplay is based around two pawn break; e6-d5 and a6-b5. Sometimes, f5 is good too, but these two are the typical ways we play against white's dominant centre. Also, we try to exploit white's dark squares so we want to exchange dark squares bishop and then just play on the dark squares. Sometimes, even move e5!? creating a massive hole on d5 can be not that dumb, but be cautious about this. I think its reasonable if you can put something on f4 or d4 quickly and get nice initiative.
Rublevsky, Sergei - Georgiev, Kiril, 0-1, YUG-chT, 1997, https://lichess.org/Gl9O64fF } { [%csl Bb2,Gd6,Be6,Bd5,Ga6,Gb5][%cal Bg7b2,Gc3d5,Be7e6,Bd6d5,Ga7a6,Gb7b5] }) (6. d4 { Sometimes played, but here I don't see a reason to be mad about this position. The most natural to me seems to get an improved Dragon Sicilian. } 6... cxd4 7. Nxd4 Nf6 8. Nc3 g6! $10 { Of course e6 is playable too, but I like this set-up. We have easy plan to play on the queen's side and I see no negatives for black here. Plan are quite similar to those you know from the Open Sicilian. In short - finish king's side castle, play a6 and b5, play Rc8 and try to play on the c-file. Maybe go for minority attack, maybe Na5-c4, maybe move the queen and play Nd7-c5. If white plays Nd5 (intending to get taken and play exd5 to then put pressure on our weak e6 pawn) we can go for Nd7 and e6. Generally, position is quite flexible and its a position which - at least in Poland - we go for if we want go get a game of "who's better wins."
Gizynski, Tomasz - Malisauskas, Vidmantas, 0-1, Katowice Fibak op, 1993, https://lichess.org/25GzgXBF } { [%csl Gg7,Gg8][%cal Gf8g7,Ge8g8] }) 6... Nf6 { Here, our idea is to just enter improved (because we traded our bad light squared bishop) French with e6 and d5 . } { [%csl Be6,Bd5][%cal Be7e6,Bd6d5] } 7. Re1 (7. d4 $36 { Interesting Gambit, but black is more than fine here } 7... Nxe4 { Its quite important that after 8. d5 Ne5 white doesn't have 9. Qa4+ winning our knight thanks to the queen on d7. } { [%csl Bd7][%cal Bd1a4,Ba4e8] } 8. d5 Ne5 { We are up a pawn, so we can live with a compromised pawn structure. If 9. Nxe5 then 9... dxe5 10. Re1 Nd6 11. Rxe5 g6! with Dragon like structure } 9. Re1 Nxf3+ 10. Qxf3 Nf6 11. c4 e5! $10 { If they take en passant then fxe6 with subsequent Be7 and O-O with complicated play. Nonetheless, we are up a pawn and are rather solid, so I don't know how white want to prove compensation here.
Gavric, Miladin - Vuckovic, Bojan, 0-1, YUG-chT2, 2000, lichess.org/oH3RjVbe
Ripari, Marcelo - Dominguez Perez, Leinier, 0-1, American Continental 2nd, 2003, lichess.org/7N6CMnas } { [%csl Ge7,Gg8][%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8] }) 7... e6 8. d4 cxd4 9. cxd4 d5! $10 { Its just a good french structure after e5. We play Ne4 and we don't have our bad light squared bishop. Also, there is no c-pawn for white so d4 is fragile. Its hard to say more that that. We have few ideas. White has is more or less obliged to play 10. e5 and then we respond with 10... Ne4. Then, we can play for the control of the c-file, maybe f6 to undermine white's centre and to create some play on the f-file. Basically, plans you have in the french without our bad bishop - what one could want more from a chess position?
Arencibia Rodriguez, Walter - Ubilava, Elizbar, 0-1, Alcobendas op, 1994, https://lichess.org/ND8nhhZa } { [%csl Be4,Gc8,Bf6][%cal Bf6e4,Ga8c8,Bf7f6] }) 2... Nf6! { This is my recommendation. I think we get pretty good game here and white often struggles for equality, but there is few EXTREMLY tricky variation here that you should know. The main problem with alapin is that its SUPER drawish, like if white wants a draw, he will get it and its quite hard to do anything about it. Also, if you don't play the best line against alapin, you will be just worse. The position you will get will be +0.4 and its not like +0.4 KID where you don't care, nah its the most painful +0.4 you could imagine. That's why, in some variations of this course, you will see drawish lines. Moreover, we need something that works well with out Nc6 sicilian cause they can play delayed alapin after 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 and our line must work with this move order too. And finally, with this line I managed to score the best draw of my life (and I had better position out of the opening). So for these reason, I decided to recommend you Nf6 mainline } 3. e5 { only critical move, if e.g. d3 Nc6 and d6 is fine for us. } 3... Nd5 { The main advantage of the approach I've chosen for this chapter is that we get in 95% positions simple and known by us positions. This is by far the most positional and drawish system white can play (it's not drawish if we don't know what to do against it). Nonetheless, I think in all lines we either get a touch more pleasent endgame or there is still some chess to be played. Here I will analyze two possible lines
1) They play d4 before Bc4
2) They play d4 after Bc4 } 4. d4 (4. Nf3 d6! { This is another huge advantage of 2... d6 move order in classical sicilian. If we were to play 2... Nc6 then we weren't be able to play this line against delayed alapin } 5. Bc4 dxe5! { Same as in the analyzed mainline } 6. Nxe5 e6 { 7. d4 cxd4 transposes. } 7. O-O Bd6! $10) 4... cxd4 5. Nf3 { Funny trick, we cannot take on c3 due to Qxd4. } 5... d6! 6. cxd4 (6. Bc4 { Against this move, we don't have dxe5. That's why we will continue in the spirit of our main move after dxe5. I think this move is recommended by levy rozman in his course. } 6... dxe5 { This is a nice idea which pretty much cuts our theory load by a third. The most dangerous and tricky lines are after main 6... Nc6, but this move just makes the whole position calm. However, if you need a win, then Nc6 is worth looking into. I think we still can win in these lines, but we don't force the issue.
We have simple plan to play e6, Nc6, maybe Nb6 and just chill. } { [%csl Be6,Gc6,Gb6][%cal Be7e6,Gb8c6,Gd5b6] } 7. Nxe5 e6 8. cxd4 (8. Qxd4 Qb6! $10 { Move worth knowing, we want to trade queens and get Nb6 with a tempo. If Bxd5 then Qxd4 cxd4 exd5 with an equal positon } { [%csl Gb6][%cal Gd5b6,Gd4b6] }) 8... Be7 9. O-O O-O 10. Nc3 Nc6! $10 { Now, we can play Bf6, if they take on c6, a5 and Ba6 is an idea. We may play Nb6 and we just play regular position } { [%csl Bf6,Gc4][%cal Be7f6,Gd5b6,Gb6c4] }) (6. Qxd4 e6 7. Nbd2 { [%csl Bd6][%cal Bd2c4,Bc4d6,Be5d6] } 7... a6! { [%csl Ge8,Bc6][%cal Gf1b5,Gb5e8,Bb8c6] } 8. Nc4 dxe5! 9. Ncxe5 f6! $15 { [%csl Be7,Bg8,Gc6][%cal Bf8e7,Be8g8,Gb8c6] }) (6. exd6 { [%csl Bd6,Bb5][%cal Bd8d6,Bb1a3,Ba3b5] } 6... e6! 7. cxd4 Bxd6 8. Nc3 Nxc3 9. bxc3 Qc7! $10 { [%csl Gg8,Bb6,Gb7,Gd7][%cal Ge8g8,Bb7b6,Gc8b7,Gb8d7] }) 6... Nc6 7. Bc4 { Here, There are two really good options for black. The first one is Nb6. The point is that we want to attack white's bishop and prepare dxe5 (so then after dxe5 Qxd1 white cannot castle.) The other nice option is dxe5. Better scoring move is dxe5 but its a lot more conrete and black must know the theory really well in order to be fine here. Also there is this annoying line dxe5 dxe5 Nbd4 0-0. There is also third move, which is e6 but after 0-0 and Qe2 (with idea Rd1) I think white has small and extremly annoying edge. } (7. Nc3?! { This move gives us simple equality. } 7... dxe5 { The point is that white will have worse pawn structure + we will trade queens. } { [%csl Gd1,Gd8][%cal Gd8d1,Gd1d8] } 8. dxe5 Nxc3 9. Qxd8+ Nxd8 10. bxc3 Bd7! { We prevent Bb5+ we will continue with g6 and Bg7 in order to attack weak e5. } { [%csl Gb5,Gg6,Ge5][%cal Gf1b5,Gg7g6,Gf8g7,Gg7e5] }) (7. exd6?! { This move was chosen by my first opponent in the tournament I mentioned earlier. I think that this move is just inaccurate. } 7... Qxd6 { We want to simply develop our pieces and castle fast. I will go along the moves from that game. } { [%csl Gg4,Gf5,Be6][%cal Gc8g4,Gc8f5,Bf8e7,Be7e6] } 8. Nc3 Bg4 9. Be2 e6! { Our bishop is behind pawn chain and there is nothing problematic about our position. } 10. O-O Be7 11. Qb3 O-O! $15 { Here I will finish the line. This is also how my game continued against that 2050 played. The point is that the pawn on b7 isn't really takable (due to various Rab8, Ndb4 and Bxf3, after these moves the pawn on d4 just hangs) and black enjoys here a nice IQP position. }) 7... dxe5! { I wanted to recommend Nb6, but there were some wierd Bxf7+ and the mainline seemed a bit too drawish. For this reason, I changed my mind. The main reason why I was hesitant about recommending dxe5 is Bxd5 as these lines are tricky. However, we get a pawn structure that should give you some flashbacks from Sveshnikov. For this, and only this reason I decided that this is fine for you. } 8. dxe5 { Main move. We have 3 decent options, I've chosen the simplest one that just equalizes.
1) If you want to play something a bit dynamic then Ndb4 is a great choice as there are many wierd Ndb4-c2 lines and its quite fun. But you must remember quite a lot of theory to not lose. Also, there is one quite annoying line which is 8... Nbd4 9. 0-0
2) If you want to play something to win, which is wierd then Be6 is fine I thought about recommending this move but in the end I wanted simplicity. But if you must win, Be6 would be my recommendation. } (8. Nxe5 { This is sometimes played but eh. } 8... e6! { with this simple move, just opening up our bishop and protecting the d5 knight, black now scores 52%. } 9. O-O Be7 { If they don't take on c6, we have typical IQP structure here } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Ge7f6,Gf6e5] } 10. Nxc6 { Sometimes they take and I love to see this move as now our knight in the centre is a monster. } 10... bxc6 11. Nc3 O-O 12. Re1 a5 $15 $140 { We will play Ba6 to trade light-squared bishops, we have semi-open b-file so we can put a rook here and generally position is quite nice for us. In lichess 2000+ database black here scores 60% } { [%csl Ga6,Bd4][%cal Gc8a6,Be7f6,Bf6d4] }) (8. Bxd5 { This line is tricky and you must know your moves. Please study this variation. } 8... Qxd5 9. Nc3 Qd6 { We must protect e5 pawn and square as white's idea d5 is strong. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gd6e5] } 10. d5 Nd4 11. Nxd4 exd4 12. Qxd4 e5! { This is the reason we must protect e5 square, now there is no Qxe5. Here, general strategy is
1) Play Bd7 to prevent any Nb5
2) Play Bf7 and 0-0. Then, depending on what white does play f6 or f5! } { [%csl Bd7,Ge7,Gg8,Bf6,Bf5][%cal Bc8d7,Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Bf7f6,Bf7f5] } 13. Qd3 { This move indicated that white wants to play Nb4. Now, after Nb4 Qb4+ doesn't fork white pieces } { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gd3b5,Gc3b5] } (13. Qe3 { Point is the same as in Qe4 line but white doesn't want to be hit with f5. The main drawback of this move is that after f4 exf4 white must take with the queen. } { [%csl Be8,Be5][%cal Be3e8,Bf2f4,Bf4e5] } 13... Be7 { We don't have to play a6 or Bd7 due to Nb5 Qb4+. Moreover, now after f4 exf4 is possible. } { [%csl Gb5,Gb4,Ge1][%cal Gc3b5,Gd6b4,Gb4b5,Gb4e1] } 14. O-O Bd7 { In this line, white has set-up in such a way we cannot achieve f5 really. For this reason, we will play something else. } { [%csl Gb5][%cal Gd7b5,Gc3b5] } 15. Re1 f6! $10 { We are rock solid, next we castle. Also, it is possible that in the future we will play f5. } { [%csl Gg8,Bf5][%cal Ge8g8,Bf6f5] }) 13... Bd7! { We stop Nb5 cause now Qb4+ indeed works. } 14. O-O f5 { These two pawns - at least to me - in some way resemble Sveshnikov and the lines after early Nd5. } { [%csl Bf4,Ge4][%cal Bf2f4,Ge5e4] } 15. Re1 Kf7! $10 { We will continue with Qf6, a6, Bd6, potentially Rc8. Our space advantage nicely restricts white, we have the bishop pair and our position is quite pleasnt to play } { [%csl Gf6,Gd6,Bc8,Ga6][%cal Gd6f6,Gf8d6,Ba8c8,Ga7a6] }) 8... Nb6! { 3) Safest and well, the most boring option which is Nb6. Why I recommend this move? Well, its simple and gives us fine endgame to play. Moreover, believe it or not black scores better than white here in every database I check (so chess.com master's database, lichess master's database and 2000+ lichess database). And to be honest, alapin is super tricky and sound, its not refutable and if we can get a simple and relaxing game against this sicilian, I am all for it. } 9. Qxd8+ Nxd8 { White has two main moves here. Our general strategy is that we want to play g6 and Bg7, then 0-0 and Nc6 attackin e5 pawn. Also, Bg4 comes in handy in many lines. } { [%csl Gg6,Ge5][%cal Gg7g6,Gf8g7,Bd8c6,Bc6e5,Gg7e5] } 10. Bb5+ { Second most popular move at amatur level and the most played move at the master level.. Here our standard g6 doesn't work so well cause this pin is really annoying and white's idea Be3-xb6 and Nc3-d5 is quite strong. } (10. Bb3 { Most popular move in lichess 2000+ database } 10... g6! { We play our idea. Now, in lichess 2000+ database about 11% of all games end in a draw here, also black's score here is about 55%. We are in the main line btw. } 11. Nc3 Bg7 12. O-O O-O! $10 { We will finish our development with Nc6, Bg4 and Rc8 and I see no issues with our position. We also have Be6 Bxe6 Nxe6. In lichess 2000+ database black's score here is 58%. } { [%csl Bg4,Ge5,Gc8,Ge6][%cal Bc8g4,Gd8c6,Gc6e5,Ga8c8,Gc8e6,Gd8e6] }) 10... Bd7 11. Bxd7+ (11. Nc3 a6 { Now Bxd7 Nxd7 transposes. } 12. Be2 Rc8! { We want to have Nc4 against Be3. We will continue with our standard g6, Bg7, 0-0 and Bg4 } { [%csl Gc4,Bg6,Bg7,Gg4][%cal Gb6c4,Bg7g6,Bf8g7,Gd7g4] }) 11... Nxd7 12. Nc3 a6! $10 { Preventing any Nb5 tricks. We will continue with e6, Be7, Nc6 and 0-0. Playing via g6 doesn't make that much sense cause we don't have Bg4. In lichess 2000+ database black's scores here 53% } *


