1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 (2. b3 { Move recommended by Levy Rozman. The point is that white will play this like Nimzo-Larsen Attack. } { [%csl Bb2,Gb5][%cal Bc1b2,Gf1b5] } 2... g6!? { The best response. We just want to counter their bishop } { [%csl Ba1][%cal Bf8g7,Bg7a1] } 3. Bb2 Nf6 { Now, our plan is to just go for the Dragon Sicilian. If they do not play e5 then simple d6 or somtimes Nc6 blocks this idea and we can continue with a good Dragon Sicilian } { [%csl Gg7,Gg8,Bc6,Bd6][%cal Gf8g7,Ge8g8,Bb8c6,Bd7d6] } 4. e5 (4. Nc3 Nc6 5. Nd5 { Sometimes Played. } 5... Bg7 { Now we have a simple idea to castle. } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 6. Nxf6+ exf6!? $10 { And here we end. We have doubled pawns, but at the same time, we have f5 to chip at white's centre. We have more development and in lichess 2000+ database blitz or slower black scores here 55% } { [%csl Bg8,Ge8,Bd6,Bf5][%cal Be8g8,Gh8e8,Bd7d6,Bf6f5] }) 4... Nd5 5. e6 (5. Qf3!? { White tries to put pressure on our position. We do not want to play e6 as this creates some serious dark squared weaknesses } { [%csl Bd5,Gh8][%cal Bf3d5,Gb2h8] } 5... Nb4! { A nice active move, we threaten now Nxc2+ } { [%csl Gc2][%cal Gb4c2] } 6. Na3 Bg7! $10 { We castle on the next move (e.g. 7. e6 O-O! =) and claim that white's pieces do not make any sense whatsoever. We may develop next with N8c6, d6 or d5 and a fine game. } { [%csl Gg8,Be5][%cal Ge8g8,Bb8c6,Bc6e5] }) 5... f6! $15 { A nice idea to shut down white's play. Next we play Bg7, O-O and we may think about e5! with massive centre. } { [%csl Bg7,Gg8][%cal Bf8g7,Ge8g8] }) (2. f4 { [%csl Rb5,Bc6][%cal Rf1b5,Bb8c6] } 2... d5! 3. exd5 (3. e5 Nc6 4. Nf3 Bg4 5. Be2 e6! $15 { [%csl Bh5,Gf5][%cal Gg8h6,Gh6f5,Bh7h5] }) 3... Nf6! { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gf6d5] } 4. c4 e6 5. dxe6 Bxe6 6. Nf3 Nc6 { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc5d4,Bc6d4,Bd8d4] } 7. Nc3 Bd6 8. d3 O-O! { [%csl Ge8,Bf5,Be7,Gd8][%cal Gf8e8,Be6f5,Bd8e7,Ga8d8] }) (2. Bc4 d6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. d3 Nc6 { [%csl Bf3,Gf4][%cal Bg1f3,Gf2f4] } 5. Nge2?! e6!? 6. O-O Be7 { [%csl Bf4,Bd5][%cal Bf2f4,Bd6d5] } 7. f4 d5! 8. Bb3 O-O 9. Ng3 dxe4! $17 { [%csl Bb6,Bb7,Gd4][%cal Bb7b6,Bc8b7,Gc6d4] }) (2. d3 Nc6 { [%csl Bb5][%cal Bf1b5] } 3. f4 g6 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Be2 d6 6. O-O Nh6!? 7. c3 O-O 8. h3 f5! $10 { [%csl Ge5,Be6][%cal Ge7e5,Be7e6] }) (2. g3 Nc6 3. Bg2 g6 4. Ne2 Bg7 5. O-O d6 6. c3 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 6... e5! 7. d3 Nge7 8. Be3 O-O! $10 { [%csl Bg4,Ge6,Gc7][%cal Bc8g4,Gc8e6,Gd8c7] }) (2. d4 cxd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 4. Qe3 g6 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Bd2 Nf6 { [%csl Ge5][%cal Be4e5,Gf6g4,Gg4e5,Gc6e5,Gg7e5] } 7. O-O-O O-O 8. f3 d5! $17) 2... d6 3. d4 (3. Nc3 { This is anti-Najdorf move order. However, as classical sicilian players, we don't care } 3... Nc6! { Here, white has pretty much two options
1) play at some point d4 and tranpose into Classical Sicilian
2) play Bb5 and go for inferior Rossolimo
Now, I wouldn't play here 3... Nf6 for one simple reason, there is a lot of theory which white wants us to go into after 4. e5. I would rather decline their offer and sidestep all of this this theoru } 4. d4 (4. Bb5 { Only move with some independent value. Here however, white gets a bad version of anti-svehsnikov } 4... e5! { We shutdown any d4 break and continue with simple moves. White would really prefer their bishop to be on c4 as then, this bishop fights for the d5 square } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Be5d4,Bc5d4] } 5. d3 Nf6 { Now, if white doesn't play Bg5, then at some point we can and should play h6. If we can get Be7 and O-O, then feel free to play h6. For the time being, I wouldn't do it as I think we don't have enough time for such a move } { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bc1g5] } 6. Bg5 Be7! { We unpin and prepare O-O. Now, they should at some point take on f6 and fight for d5 as otherwise, their set-up doesn't make much sense } { [%csl Gf6,Gg8,Ba6,Bb5][%cal Gd8f6,Ge8g8,Ba7a6,Bb7b5] } 7. Bxf6 (7. O-O O-O 8. h3 Be6! $10 { Here, we can expand with a6-b5, we have ideas of Nd4 or Nd7 to trade our bad bishop. Also, we can insist on the Bxf6 move with h6. Generally speaking, position is quite closed and we should think about pawn breaks. Therefore, we have ideas of f5 (or g6 and f5) in some position too. } { [%csl Bd4,Ba6,Bb5,Gd7,Gh6][%cal Bc6d4,Ba7a6,Bb7b5,Gf6d7,Gh7h6] }) 7... Bxf6 8. Nd5 O-O 9. O-O Bg4! $10 { And here, I would end the line. We have ideas of a6-b5 to grab some space on the queen's side. Also, now Nd4 is quite a threat and g6 with Bg7 and on play on the king's side is still on the table. } { [%csl Bd4,Ga6,Gb5,Bg6,Bg7][%cal Bc6d4,Ga7a6,Gb7b5,Bf6g7,Bg7g6] }) 4... cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nf6! { And we are back into the Classical Sicilian }) (3. Bc4 { Some new wierd Italian - Sicilian Offspring. } 3... Nf6 { Most sensible move, to attack the e4 pawn. If 4. e5 dxe5 5. Nxe5 e6! (protects f7 pawn) 6. O-O Bd6 and then Qc7, maybe a6-b5 idea etc. Position is quite simple to play. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf6e4] } 4. d3 Nc6! { White won't play d4 now (I mean, they can but will lose a tempo) so we make sure e5 is no longer an issue as we want to go for a line, which you know by know quite well. } { [%csl Ge5][%cal Gc6e5] } 5. O-O g6! { And once again, we play the Dragon Like position. Its quite amusing how we can play this against virtually anything, } 6. c3 Bg7 7. Bg5 O-O! $10 { Here we will end. Few ideas to note, mostly h6 to kick away white's bishop, Na5 to win the bishop pair, b6 (or a6-b5) and Bb7 to have double finachetto. Another idea is to break in the centre with e6-d5 break, which is quite thematic in the both Italian (the d5 break itself) and Closed Sicilian. } { [%csl Bh6,Ge6,Gd5,Bb6,Bb7,Ba5][%cal Bh7h6,Ge7e6,Gd6d5,Bb7b6,Bc8b7,Bc6a5] }) 3... cxd4 4. Qxd4 { Played by people who are either bored with open sicilian or are scared to enter it due to theory } 4... Nc6 5. Bb5 a6! { Seems to me to be the simplest option. We will just get a structure known for Sveshnikov players as "white is a moron". } 6. Bxc6+ bxc6 7. Nc3 (7. c4 e5! { And that's pretty much it. We get a Boleslavsky Hole Structure, but without a hole. We quite literally eat a cookie and get a cookie } 8. Qd3 Ne7!? { Interesting idea to play for f5. We can expect white won't play O-O-O after c4, therefore we go for optimal set-up centred around king's side expansion via f5. Of course there is nothing wrong with Nf6, Be7, h6 (prevents Bg5) and O-O. If this is hard for you to remember, don't worry as you can play in the same way you do against the 7. Nc3 line. } { [%csl Gg5,Gf6,Bg6][%cal Gc1g5,Gf7f6,Be7g6] } 9. Nc3 Ng6! $15 { Next we can O-O, Be7 and f5!. We can wait too and I have no idea what's white plan. I mean, c5 seems the most logical option, but they cannot achieve it. Meanwhile we have the b-file and easy to follow plan } { [%csl Ge7,Gg8,Bf5][%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Bf7f5,Bc8f5] }) 7... e5 8. Qd3 Nf6! { Not Ne7-g6 as they haven't commited to c4, therefore still have an option to go for O-O-O. Therefore we stick to the regular stuff. } 9. Bg5 h6! $15 { [%csl Ge6,Bh4,Bg5,Ge7,Gg8,Ba5,Ba6][%cal Gc8e6,Bg5h4,Bg7g5,Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Ba6a5,Bc8a6] } 